1
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:05,520
Hello,

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:08,240
today we will explain the system aspects

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:10,320
of computed tomography. Since the CT

4
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:12,719
data acquisition is a scanning process,

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:15,719
a CT system is typically called a CT

6
00:00:12,719 --> 00:00:15,719
scanner.

7
00:00:17,199 --> 00:00:21,600
As you can see, the topic of this

8
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:24,640
lecture is called CT scanner. Next time

9
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:26,320
we will have a mat lab session.

10
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:29,679
During that, we'll talk about filtered

11
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:31,359
back projection and related concepts.

12
00:00:29,679 --> 00:00:33,360
You'll have the opportunity to review

13
00:00:31,359 --> 00:00:37,719
mat lab commands and functions so you

14
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:37,719
can perform filtered back projection.

15
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:42,960
So let's look at the outline for today's

16
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:44,800
lecture.

17
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:46,640
First we'll talk about projection data

18
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:48,640
truncation. The issue when the x-ray

19
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:51,360
projections don't fully cover the object

20
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:52,960
which can cause artifacts.

21
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:56,000
Next we'll discuss different scanning

22
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:58,000
modes used in CT technology.

23
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,840
Then we will move on to common image

24
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:01,680
artifacts that can degrade image

25
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:03,760
quality.

26
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:05,680
Finally, we'll cover X-ray radiation

27
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:07,680
dose, which is important because it can

28
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:10,680
be harmful to patients if not properly

29
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:10,680
managed.

30
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:16,640
As we discussed in the previous lecture,

31
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:18,960
when you collect X-ray projection data

32
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:21,200
from an unimpeded perspective, you aim

33
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:23,920
to reconstruct the image layer by layer,

34
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:25,920
starting from the outermost shell.

35
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:27,600
It's like peeling an onion, gradually

36
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:29,360
revealing each layer beneath the

37
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:31,520
surface.

38
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:33,439
To achieve this, you need to have X-ray

39
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:36,000
measurements along every line passing

40
00:01:33,439 --> 00:01:37,759
through the region of interest.

41
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,759
For each X-ray, you want to record the

42
00:01:37,759 --> 00:01:41,920
line integral, the total attenuation

43
00:01:39,759 --> 00:01:44,240
along that path.

44
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:46,720
This situation pertains to 2D image

45
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:50,079
reconstruction. Always keep in mind the

46
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:51,920
principle of unimpeded data acquisition.

47
00:01:50,079 --> 00:01:54,399
We say there must be at least one point

48
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:56,799
on the X-ray source trajectory for every

49
00:01:54,399 --> 00:01:58,320
line passing through the object. In

50
00:01:56,799 --> 00:02:00,320
other words, you need a measured

51
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:02,799
projection corresponding to every line

52
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:04,880
through the cross-section.

53
00:02:02,799 --> 00:02:08,360
This ensures you have complete data for

54
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:08,360
accurate reconstruction.

55
00:02:10,239 --> 00:02:15,280
Now in the 3DCT case, the X-ray source

56
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:17,520
remains a point source, but instead of

57
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:19,440
using a linear detector array, you use a

58
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:21,120
planer or two-dimensional detector

59
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:23,120
array.

60
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:25,360
This configuration results in a cone

61
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:28,000
beam geometry originating from the point

62
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:30,400
source.

63
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,560
The X-ray source emits radiation in a

64
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:35,200
cone-shaped beam, which should be wide

65
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:37,040
enough to cover the entire object. In

66
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:39,280
other words, the object must be fully

67
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:42,160
contained within the cone beam to ensure

68
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:44,560
complete data acquisition.

69
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:47,920
This concept is similar to the 2D case

70
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:49,599
but extended to volutric imaging.

71
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:51,840
Once you have all the rays, as shown

72
00:02:49,599 --> 00:02:54,000
here, you can rearrange, reorganize, or

73
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:56,720
rebin the X-rays into parallel beam

74
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,959
geometry. In parallel beam geometry, you

75
00:02:56,720 --> 00:03:00,560
can apply Forier transform-based imaging

76
00:02:58,959 --> 00:03:03,040
methods.

77
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:05,519
The Forier slice theorem allows the use

78
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:07,280
of the one-dimensional forier transform

79
00:03:05,519 --> 00:03:10,159
on projections to recover the

80
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:12,400
two-dimensional forier spectrum. Each

81
00:03:10,159 --> 00:03:14,560
projection recovers a profile in forier

82
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:16,239
space and by changing the viewing angle,

83
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:19,920
you sample different radial lines

84
00:03:16,239 --> 00:03:22,239
sweeping over the entire forier domain.

85
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:24,239
Likewise, in 3D imaging, an extended

86
00:03:22,239 --> 00:03:26,959
version of the Forier slice theorem is

87
00:03:24,239 --> 00:03:28,720
required. This involves applying Forier

88
00:03:26,959 --> 00:03:30,879
transforms in higher dimensions to

89
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:34,760
recover the 3D spatial frequency

90
00:03:30,879 --> 00:03:34,760
information of the object.

91
00:03:36,159 --> 00:03:41,360
The Fier transform as we learned is the

92
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:43,440
foundation of classic CT theory. It

93
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:46,319
requires complete coverage of an entire

94
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:48,159
cross-section or the whole object.

95
00:03:46,319 --> 00:03:51,280
When you have projection data, you can

96
00:03:48,159 --> 00:03:53,360
use the Forier slice theorem in 2D or 3D

97
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:56,319
and then perform the inverse forier

98
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:57,840
transform also in 2D or 3D. This

99
00:03:56,319 --> 00:03:59,920
classical framework is called the

100
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:02,400
central dogma.

101
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:05,040
The whole cross-section or entire object

102
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:06,720
must be fully covered by the X-ray beam.

103
00:04:05,040 --> 00:04:09,040
No matter the shape of the beam, you can

104
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:11,599
always rebin the data into parallel beam

105
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:13,439
geometry. Then the collected data can be

106
00:04:11,599 --> 00:04:15,680
inversely transformed back to the

107
00:04:13,439 --> 00:04:17,680
original image domain. That's the

108
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:20,639
fundamental way image reconstruction is

109
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:22,560
performed.

110
00:04:20,639 --> 00:04:24,720
As we learned, filtered back projection

111
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:26,479
is essentially just the inverse forier

112
00:04:24,720 --> 00:04:28,880
transform represented in polar

113
00:04:26,479 --> 00:04:31,360
coordinates. This core concept explains

114
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:33,360
the process pretty well.

115
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:35,759
If you follow this, you should grasp the

116
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:37,360
essential idea. In my opinion, the key

117
00:04:35,759 --> 00:04:39,919
question is what is the difference

118
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:42,919
between classic CT theory and modern CT

119
00:04:39,919 --> 00:04:42,919
theory?

120
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:48,800
This leads us to the issue of data

121
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:51,040
truncation. If the data is incomplete,

122
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:53,040
the forier slice theorem, which assumes

123
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:55,680
a full projection profile for each

124
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:58,720
one-dimensional forier transform, can no

125
00:04:55,680 --> 00:05:00,320
longer be applied straightforwardly.

126
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:02,960
What happens if part of the data is

127
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:04,560
missing?

128
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:06,240
If some portion of the projection

129
00:05:04,560 --> 00:05:07,759
profile is lost, the forier

130
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:09,600
transformation cannot be performed

131
00:05:07,759 --> 00:05:12,240
fully. This is called the data

132
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:13,840
truncation problem. Much of modern CT

133
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:16,160
theory has been developed to address

134
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:18,240
this data truncation problem. Finding

135
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:20,560
ways to reconstruct images even with

136
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:22,560
incomplete data.

137
00:05:20,560 --> 00:05:23,919
Before we delve into the types of data

138
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:26,919
truncation, let me give you some

139
00:05:23,919 --> 00:05:26,919
context.

140
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:32,240
First, let me show you the four

141
00:05:29,919 --> 00:05:34,880
generations of CT scanning geometry,

142
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:36,080
often called generations or modes. This

143
00:05:34,880 --> 00:05:38,160
basically refers to how you

144
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:40,720
systematically collect X-ray projection

145
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:42,320
data.

146
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:44,240
In the first generation, you have a

147
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:46,639
single point X-ray source and a single

148
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:49,199
point detector. Each measurement records

149
00:05:46,639 --> 00:05:50,960
one line, one line integral. The setup

150
00:05:49,199 --> 00:05:52,639
moves by translation, producing one

151
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:54,400
parallel beam projection in one

152
00:05:52,639 --> 00:05:56,479
direction.

153
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:58,320
Then the entire apparatus rotates

154
00:05:56,479 --> 00:06:00,000
slightly to a new angular position,

155
00:05:58,320 --> 00:06:02,479
producing another parallel beam

156
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,720
projection. This process depends on the

157
00:06:02,479 --> 00:06:07,199
viewing angle, often labeled theta.

158
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:08,880
That's the first generation.

159
00:06:07,199 --> 00:06:11,039
The second generation improves data

160
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:13,440
acquisition efficiency. Instead of

161
00:06:11,039 --> 00:06:15,759
measuring one line integral at a time,

162
00:06:13,440 --> 00:06:18,160
you use a small detector array with a

163
00:06:15,759 --> 00:06:20,160
narrow fan beam.

164
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:22,560
This detector array collects multiple

165
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:25,680
lines simultaneously as it translates,

166
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:27,680
covering the cross-section completely.

167
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:29,680
Each translation collects multiple

168
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:32,160
parallel beam projections depending on

169
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:33,840
how many detector shells you have. For

170
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:35,840
example, if you have five detector

171
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:38,400
shells, you get five parallel beam

172
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:42,800
projections per translation. After a

173
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:44,720
small rotation, you repeat this process.

174
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:47,120
The third generation further improves

175
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:49,199
efficiency dramatically. Instead of a

176
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:51,680
few detector elements, you have hundreds

177
00:06:49,199 --> 00:06:54,639
or thousands along a linear or arcshaped

178
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:57,039
wide detector array. This is a full fan

179
00:06:54,639 --> 00:06:59,120
beam imaging geometry. The detector

180
00:06:57,039 --> 00:07:00,720
array continuously rotates to collect

181
00:06:59,120 --> 00:07:02,880
data.

182
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:05,039
This configuration satisfies the data

183
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:08,479
sufficiency condition. Performing a full

184
00:07:05,039 --> 00:07:10,160
360° scan ensures every line through the

185
00:07:08,479 --> 00:07:11,919
patients cross-section is measured

186
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:13,599
somewhere so the line integrals are

187
00:07:11,919 --> 00:07:15,440
known.

188
00:07:13,599 --> 00:07:18,639
This third generation geometry is still

189
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:20,800
very popular. Modern CT scanners mostly

190
00:07:18,639 --> 00:07:22,880
use this geometry but have expanded the

191
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:26,160
one-dimensional detector arrays into

192
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:29,680
multirow or cone beam configurations.

193
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:31,759
We'll discuss those later.

194
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:33,759
The fourth generation differs by making

195
00:07:31,759 --> 00:07:36,479
the detector array a full stationary

196
00:07:33,759 --> 00:07:38,560
ring. Instead of rotating the detectors,

197
00:07:36,479 --> 00:07:40,160
only the X-ray source rotates around the

198
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:41,919
patient.

199
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:44,479
The detector ring stays fixed,

200
00:07:41,919 --> 00:07:46,880
continuously collecting signals. This

201
00:07:44,479 --> 00:07:48,720
fourth generation geometry solves some

202
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:52,919
mechanical problems associated with

203
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:52,919
rotating the detector array.

204
00:07:54,479 --> 00:07:58,879
Next, we have electron beam CT, which

205
00:07:56,560 --> 00:08:01,120
uses a different approach. Instead of a

206
00:07:58,879 --> 00:08:03,599
mechanical X-ray tube rotation like

207
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:05,599
earlier generations, this system uses an

208
00:08:03,599 --> 00:08:07,680
electron beam that's electromagnetically

209
00:08:05,599 --> 00:08:10,240
steered to hit a fixed target shaped as

210
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:12,560
multiple metallic arcs.

211
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:15,039
Each arc produces X-rays upward, forming

212
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:17,199
the scanning beam. The electron beam is

213
00:08:15,039 --> 00:08:18,800
steered by electromagnetic fields very

214
00:08:17,199 --> 00:08:20,879
rapidly without moving parts

215
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:23,440
mechanically. This allows for much

216
00:08:20,879 --> 00:08:25,360
faster imaging speeds.

217
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:27,440
The driving force behind the evolution

218
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:29,840
of scanning generations is increasing

219
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:31,759
data acquisition efficiency, collecting

220
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:35,039
the same amount of data in a shorter

221
00:08:31,759 --> 00:08:37,279
time to improve temporal resolution.

222
00:08:35,039 --> 00:08:39,680
Cardiac imaging is the most challenging

223
00:08:37,279 --> 00:08:42,800
application for CT since the heart beats

224
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:45,200
fast, anywhere between 60 and 100 beats

225
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:47,200
per minute. Tomographic reconstruction

226
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:49,920
assumes the object is stationary, but

227
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:51,519
the heart is constantly moving. So we

228
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:53,760
need to shorten the data acquisition

229
00:08:51,519 --> 00:08:55,760
time drastically.

230
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:57,440
The electron beam CT scanner was

231
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:59,839
designed specifically for cardiac

232
00:08:57,440 --> 00:09:01,920
imaging due to its high speed. However,

233
00:08:59,839 --> 00:09:04,000
it is costly and image noise is often

234
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:06,240
higher. Additionally, the fourth

235
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,240
generation geometry faces problems with

236
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:10,640
scattered signals affecting other

237
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:13,839
detectors which causes degradation in

238
00:09:10,640 --> 00:09:15,920
image quality.

239
00:09:13,839 --> 00:09:18,320
Because of these limitations, the third

240
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:21,519
generation geometry remains more widely

241
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:25,800
used today and electron beam CT scanners

242
00:09:21,519 --> 00:09:25,800
are now mostly considered outdated.

243
00:09:27,680 --> 00:09:31,920
Defining CT scanning modes or how many

244
00:09:29,839 --> 00:09:34,000
generations to count varies depending on

245
00:09:31,920 --> 00:09:36,640
the source. Different textbooks

246
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,560
categorize them differently.

247
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:40,560
Usually the first three generations are

248
00:09:38,560 --> 00:09:42,399
well established, the first generation,

249
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:44,399
the second generation and the third

250
00:09:42,399 --> 00:09:46,480
generation.

251
00:09:44,399 --> 00:09:48,240
However, beyond these the classification

252
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:51,480
becomes a matter of opinion and varies

253
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:51,480
by source.

254
00:09:53,040 --> 00:09:56,800
Let's look at scanning modes. Here you

255
00:09:55,279 --> 00:09:58,560
can visualize a diamond that helps you

256
00:09:56,800 --> 00:10:01,519
remember the progression of CT

257
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:03,920
generations. The first generation uses a

258
00:10:01,519 --> 00:10:05,680
parallel beam, then a narrow fan beam in

259
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:08,080
the second generation, followed by a

260
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:10,080
wide fan beam with rotation in the third

261
00:10:08,080 --> 00:10:12,160
generation.

262
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:14,800
The third generation features fan beam

263
00:10:12,160 --> 00:10:16,160
geometry with rotating detectors. Beyond

264
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:18,560
that, we have the fourth and fifth

265
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:20,320
generations. The next stages are called

266
00:10:18,560 --> 00:10:22,880
the sixth or seventh generation, which

267
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:24,880
involve helical scanning.

268
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:27,839
Helical scanning is quite an innovative

269
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:30,240
out-ofthe- box idea. Traditionally, with

270
00:10:27,839 --> 00:10:32,320
one-dimensional detector arrays, imaging

271
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:34,959
is done by rotating the source around

272
00:10:32,320 --> 00:10:37,279
the patient, often a half circle or full

273
00:10:34,959 --> 00:10:39,440
circle, to collect sufficient data for

274
00:10:37,279 --> 00:10:41,440
reconstructing one cross-section using

275
00:10:39,440 --> 00:10:44,160
filtered back projection or iterative

276
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:46,079
reconstruction algorithms.

277
00:10:44,160 --> 00:10:48,240
After reconstructing one slice, the

278
00:10:46,079 --> 00:10:50,640
patient table is translated slightly to

279
00:10:48,240 --> 00:10:52,640
image the next cross-section. This

280
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:54,240
process is known as the step andoot

281
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:56,320
mode.

282
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:58,320
You scan one cross-section, then move

283
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:59,920
the patient, accelerate or decelerate

284
00:10:58,320 --> 00:11:02,320
the table, perform another full

285
00:10:59,920 --> 00:11:04,079
rotation, and repeat.

286
00:11:02,320 --> 00:11:05,920
This approach is time consuming,

287
00:11:04,079 --> 00:11:08,079
especially because anatomical and

288
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:10,399
pathological features exist in three

289
00:11:08,079 --> 00:11:13,200
dimensions, and patients can only hold

290
00:11:10,399 --> 00:11:15,200
still for limited times. Scanning slice

291
00:11:13,200 --> 00:11:17,519
by slice leads to low throughput and

292
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:19,279
motion artifacts.

293
00:11:17,519 --> 00:11:21,440
Spiral scanning solves many of these

294
00:11:19,279 --> 00:11:23,680
issues. You ask the patient to remain

295
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:25,440
still, often holding their breath. Then

296
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:27,360
the table moves continuously while the

297
00:11:25,440 --> 00:11:29,680
X-ray source rotates and data

298
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:31,680
acquisition happens simultaneously and

299
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:33,200
continuously.

300
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:35,120
From the patients perspective, the

301
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:37,040
source follows a helical trajectory

302
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:39,839
which is why this mode is called helical

303
00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:41,680
imaging.

304
00:11:39,839 --> 00:11:44,079
However, helical scanning introduces

305
00:11:41,680 --> 00:11:46,079
data truncation issues. In second

306
00:11:44,079 --> 00:11:48,240
generation scanning, the data may be

307
00:11:46,079 --> 00:11:50,399
transversely truncated due to the small

308
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:52,160
detector size.

309
00:11:50,399 --> 00:11:54,240
In helical scanning, data is

310
00:11:52,160 --> 00:11:56,560
longitudinally truncated because the

311
00:11:54,240 --> 00:11:58,480
source follows a helical path within any

312
00:11:56,560 --> 00:12:00,240
transverse plane, meaning a full

313
00:11:58,480 --> 00:12:04,120
two-dimensional data set for image

314
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:04,120
reconstruction is absent.

315
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:09,600
This slide illustrates a spiral single

316
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:11,920
slice CT scan showing the imaging

317
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:15,360
geometry. You see the helical path

318
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:17,600
around the patient clearly.

319
00:12:15,360 --> 00:12:19,519
Now the question is how do we perform

320
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:21,680
image reconstruction effectively despite

321
00:12:19,519 --> 00:12:23,519
this geometry?

322
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:27,240
How do we handle the challenges posed by

323
00:12:23,519 --> 00:12:27,240
the helical scan?

324
00:12:28,639 --> 00:12:32,959
With helical scanning when you want to

325
00:12:30,320 --> 00:12:35,120
reconstruct an XY cross-section ideally

326
00:12:32,959 --> 00:12:37,440
you'd have rays confined entirely within

327
00:12:35,120 --> 00:12:39,760
the XY plane but the actual green

328
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:42,560
helical scanning path deviates from this

329
00:12:39,760 --> 00:12:44,639
ideal.

330
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:46,639
You measure line integrals only along

331
00:12:44,639 --> 00:12:49,440
rays that coincide with this helical

332
00:12:46,639 --> 00:12:51,279
path. Immediately the x-ray source moves

333
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:53,279
out of the plane producing rays above

334
00:12:51,279 --> 00:12:55,440
and below it.

335
00:12:53,279 --> 00:12:57,839
To reconstruct a cross-section say in

336
00:12:55,440 --> 00:12:59,920
the xy plane you need every line

337
00:12:57,839 --> 00:13:03,920
integral through this section including

338
00:12:59,920 --> 00:13:05,920
along particular rays such as ray a.

339
00:13:03,920 --> 00:13:08,079
In practice you can't measure the line

340
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:09,920
integral exactly along ray a because the

341
00:13:08,079 --> 00:13:12,000
source never stays on that plane long

342
00:13:09,920 --> 00:13:13,839
enough. you only have rays above and

343
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:18,240
below it. That's the central data

344
00:13:13,839 --> 00:13:20,399
truncation problem in helical CT.

345
00:13:18,240 --> 00:13:21,920
Engineers found a practical solution.

346
00:13:20,399 --> 00:13:24,000
Helical scanning benefits from

347
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:26,560
continuous table motion without stops or

348
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:28,480
accelerations. The patient lies flat on

349
00:13:26,560 --> 00:13:30,720
the table which moves steadily through

350
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:32,720
the scanner. The patient simply stays

351
00:13:30,720 --> 00:13:35,920
still until done, making the process

352
00:13:32,720 --> 00:13:37,920
fast and smooth.

353
00:13:35,920 --> 00:13:40,240
To compensate for missing data, we

354
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:42,000
perform linear interpolation between the

355
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:44,320
upper and lower rays which are

356
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:46,399
available. The assumption is that the

357
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:48,560
data varies roughly linearly between

358
00:13:46,399 --> 00:13:51,120
these rays.

359
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:53,440
This allows synthesis of an inplane data

360
00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:56,240
set which then can be used with filtered

361
00:13:53,440 --> 00:13:58,639
back projection algorithms. This was the

362
00:13:56,240 --> 00:14:00,800
first trial approach.

363
00:13:58,639 --> 00:14:03,360
Later improvements involved helical

364
00:14:00,800 --> 00:14:05,040
scanning, which uses opposite rays, not

365
00:14:03,360 --> 00:14:07,360
just the nearest rays in the same

366
00:14:05,040 --> 00:14:09,120
direction. Before reaching a certain

367
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:11,120
position, you could use complimentary

368
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:14,600
rays shot from an opposite angle to

369
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:14,600
improve interpolation.

370
00:14:16,639 --> 00:14:20,959
Using opposite rays that are closer to

371
00:14:18,720 --> 00:14:23,440
ray A reduces the distance between the

372
00:14:20,959 --> 00:14:25,199
measured and estimated line integrals.

373
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:27,760
The narrower this gap, the better the

374
00:14:25,199 --> 00:14:30,160
accuracy you can expect.

375
00:14:27,760 --> 00:14:32,639
This half scan interpolation enhances

376
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:34,800
longitudinal resolution along the Z-axis

377
00:14:32,639 --> 00:14:36,800
compared to linear interpolation.

378
00:14:34,800 --> 00:14:38,560
Longitudinal resolution along the Z

379
00:14:36,800 --> 00:14:40,399
direction is often lower than inplane

380
00:14:38,560 --> 00:14:42,399
resolution.

381
00:14:40,399 --> 00:14:44,560
Back when I just graduated and started

382
00:14:42,399 --> 00:14:47,360
work at Washington University Medical

383
00:14:44,560 --> 00:14:49,920
School in Saint Louis Hel CT was

384
00:14:47,360 --> 00:14:54,000
introduced, radiologists appreciated the

385
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:56,079
improved temporal resolution it offered.

386
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:57,760
However, they noticed motion blurring

387
00:14:56,079 --> 00:15:00,320
along the Z-axis caused by the

388
00:14:57,760 --> 00:15:02,079
interpolation process. Since the patient

389
00:15:00,320 --> 00:15:04,399
is continuously moved through the

390
00:15:02,079 --> 00:15:06,959
scanner, motion blur appears in the

391
00:15:04,399 --> 00:15:09,680
longitudinal direction, similar to a

392
00:15:06,959 --> 00:15:11,440
photo taken while running.

393
00:15:09,680 --> 00:15:13,760
As they say, there's no free lunch. You

394
00:15:11,440 --> 00:15:16,000
gain temporal resolution but lose some

395
00:15:13,760 --> 00:15:19,000
longitudinal resolution due to motion

396
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,000
blur.

397
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:25,120
We analyzed Helico CT scanning modes

398
00:15:22,560 --> 00:15:27,760
further. Traditional step-and-shoot CT

399
00:15:25,120 --> 00:15:30,000
uses multiple circular scans. For each

400
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:33,199
scan, you reconstruct one CT slice

401
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:35,519
repeating many times for full coverage.

402
00:15:33,199 --> 00:15:37,600
In helical CT, instead of discrete

403
00:15:35,519 --> 00:15:40,160
circular scans, you have one continuous

404
00:15:37,600 --> 00:15:41,839
helical acquisition. For each helical

405
00:15:40,160 --> 00:15:44,480
rotation, interpolation creates

406
00:15:41,839 --> 00:15:46,399
synthetic data points.

407
00:15:44,480 --> 00:15:48,720
You can reconstruct images at many

408
00:15:46,399 --> 00:15:50,720
arbitrary longitudinal positions along

409
00:15:48,720 --> 00:15:53,120
the patient, generating a large number

410
00:15:50,720 --> 00:15:54,720
of slices computationally. This

411
00:15:53,120 --> 00:15:56,880
technique is called retrospective

412
00:15:54,720 --> 00:15:58,800
reconstruction.

413
00:15:56,880 --> 00:16:00,880
Retrospective reconstruction has a

414
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:03,199
significant clinical advantage. For

415
00:16:00,880 --> 00:16:05,120
example, in tumor detection, a lesion

416
00:16:03,199 --> 00:16:08,480
may fall between two slices in

417
00:16:05,120 --> 00:16:10,240
traditional scanning and go undetected.

418
00:16:08,480 --> 00:16:12,480
With retrospective reconstruction,

419
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:14,079
densely sampled slices mean one slice

420
00:16:12,480 --> 00:16:16,399
will include the tumor, improving

421
00:16:14,079 --> 00:16:19,399
detection confidence and diagnostic

422
00:16:16,399 --> 00:16:19,399
accuracy.

423
00:16:21,279 --> 00:16:25,040
Next, we have partial volume averaging

424
00:16:23,199 --> 00:16:26,959
artifacts

425
00:16:25,040 --> 00:16:29,040
for each slice. If a tumor only

426
00:16:26,959 --> 00:16:31,519
partially lies within that slice, you

427
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:33,279
might not see it clearly. Small tumors

428
00:16:31,519 --> 00:16:35,839
can be especially difficult to detect

429
00:16:33,279 --> 00:16:38,000
when they occupy only half of a slice

430
00:16:35,839 --> 00:16:41,399
and image noise further complicates

431
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:41,399
clear visualization.

432
00:16:41,600 --> 00:16:46,160
However, with spiral CT, you can select

433
00:16:43,759 --> 00:16:48,240
longitudinal positions as densely as you

434
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:51,680
want. This gives you a real opportunity

435
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:53,680
to reconstruct images densely enough.

436
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:55,279
One of the reconstructed slices is

437
00:16:53,680 --> 00:16:56,800
likely to contain the tumor fully,

438
00:16:55,279 --> 00:17:00,600
providing much better contrast,

439
00:16:56,800 --> 00:17:00,600
resolution, and visibility.

440
00:17:02,240 --> 00:17:05,760
Building on this huristic idea, we

441
00:17:04,160 --> 00:17:07,679
performed a rigorous linear system

442
00:17:05,760 --> 00:17:10,160
analysis and published a journal paper

443
00:17:07,679 --> 00:17:11,839
in medical physics.

444
00:17:10,160 --> 00:17:14,000
We performed modeling the signal

445
00:17:11,839 --> 00:17:15,679
response of the CT system to compare the

446
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:18,000
two scanning modes. As I mentioned

447
00:17:15,679 --> 00:17:20,079
earlier in the lecture, that is multiple

448
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:23,360
scanning circles versus a spiral or

449
00:17:20,079 --> 00:17:25,199
helical scanning trajectory. Our key

450
00:17:23,360 --> 00:17:27,199
theoretical conclusion is that for a

451
00:17:25,199 --> 00:17:29,120
given X-ray dose, meaning a fixed number

452
00:17:27,199 --> 00:17:30,559
of total incident photons, you can

453
00:17:29,120 --> 00:17:32,400
choose to scan in either the

454
00:17:30,559 --> 00:17:34,400
conventional step and shoot mode or

455
00:17:32,400 --> 00:17:38,000
helical mode to have multiple scanning

456
00:17:34,400 --> 00:17:40,559
circles or a single helical trajectory.

457
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:42,400
Given the same X-ray dose, Hel CT offers

458
00:17:40,559 --> 00:17:44,240
substantially better longitudinal

459
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:45,760
resolution than conventional step and

460
00:17:44,240 --> 00:17:47,600
shoot.

461
00:17:45,760 --> 00:17:49,760
This improvement comes from the inherent

462
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:52,000
capability of helical CT to perform

463
00:17:49,760 --> 00:17:54,400
retrospective reconstruction that is

464
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:57,200
reconstructed overlapping slices along

465
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:58,720
the longitudinal axis.

466
00:17:57,200 --> 00:18:00,640
In our papers, we specifically

467
00:17:58,720 --> 00:18:02,880
recommended reconstructing roughly three

468
00:18:00,640 --> 00:18:05,840
to four slices per helical turn for the

469
00:18:02,880 --> 00:18:08,000
best results.

470
00:18:05,840 --> 00:18:10,160
Thus, helical CT not only improves

471
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:12,480
temporal resolution but also enhances

472
00:18:10,160 --> 00:18:15,039
longitudinal resolution so there's no

473
00:18:12,480 --> 00:18:16,880
loss in image quality. This important

474
00:18:15,039 --> 00:18:19,679
finding helps explain why helical

475
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:21,440
scanning is so beneficial.

476
00:18:19,679 --> 00:18:23,440
Some believe that you gain temporal

477
00:18:21,440 --> 00:18:26,720
resolution with helical scanning but

478
00:18:23,440 --> 00:18:28,640
lose longitudinal resolution.

479
00:18:26,720 --> 00:18:30,559
However, with proper overlapping

480
00:18:28,640 --> 00:18:33,360
reconstruction, both temporal and

481
00:18:30,559 --> 00:18:35,200
longitudinal resolution can be improved.

482
00:18:33,360 --> 00:18:36,880
This is a key insight from our earlier

483
00:18:35,200 --> 00:18:40,280
work and kind of a free lunch in

484
00:18:36,880 --> 00:18:40,280
clinical practice.

485
00:18:41,840 --> 00:18:47,280
Hel or spiral CT began with single slice

486
00:18:45,039 --> 00:18:50,559
scanning using a linear detector array

487
00:18:47,280 --> 00:18:52,640
capturing rays in two dimensions.

488
00:18:50,559 --> 00:18:55,520
Expanding this cone beam helical spiral

489
00:18:52,640 --> 00:18:57,679
CT was developed. For cone beam spiral

490
00:18:55,520 --> 00:19:00,160
CT the issue of data truncation is

491
00:18:57,679 --> 00:19:01,840
addressed more intricately.

492
00:19:00,160 --> 00:19:04,080
If you slice through the patient in any

493
00:19:01,840 --> 00:19:06,160
plane the actual X-ray beam forms a fan

494
00:19:04,080 --> 00:19:07,919
beam in that plane.

495
00:19:06,160 --> 00:19:10,000
This fan beam is longitudinally

496
00:19:07,919 --> 00:19:12,400
truncated because the patient's body is

497
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:14,559
quite long and the detector size limits

498
00:19:12,400 --> 00:19:18,799
the coverage. You cannot cover the whole

499
00:19:14,559 --> 00:19:20,960
body with a single cone beam at once.

500
00:19:18,799 --> 00:19:23,200
Often you focus on smaller regions of

501
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:26,799
interest like the heart or inner ear. So

502
00:19:23,200 --> 00:19:29,120
using a huge cone beam isn't practical.

503
00:19:26,799 --> 00:19:31,600
Consequently, data truncation naturally

504
00:19:29,120 --> 00:19:33,679
occurs in helical cone beam scanning

505
00:19:31,600 --> 00:19:37,440
making it an important consideration in

506
00:19:33,679 --> 00:19:37,440
advanced CT imaging.

507
00:19:39,280 --> 00:19:43,039
In the early 1990s, I wrote a paper

508
00:19:41,520 --> 00:19:45,919
called the general cone beam

509
00:19:43,039 --> 00:19:48,080
reconstruction algorithm.

510
00:19:45,919 --> 00:19:49,840
It explains that for cone beam helical

511
00:19:48,080 --> 00:19:51,760
scanning where the beam geometry is

512
00:19:49,840 --> 00:19:54,000
divergent, you cannot simply apply

513
00:19:51,760 --> 00:19:55,760
linear interpolation to reconstruct

514
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:57,520
images.

515
00:19:55,760 --> 00:19:59,280
The mathematics involved is more

516
00:19:57,520 --> 00:20:01,440
complicated, requiring specialized

517
00:19:59,280 --> 00:20:05,400
algorithms to handle data truncation and

518
00:20:01,440 --> 00:20:05,400
beam geometry correctly.

519
00:20:06,960 --> 00:20:10,960
This particular paper became the first

520
00:20:08,960 --> 00:20:14,480
seminal work on helical cone beam

521
00:20:10,960 --> 00:20:16,960
scanning and has been widely cited.

522
00:20:14,480 --> 00:20:19,760
Today multi slice cone beam helical

523
00:20:16,960 --> 00:20:22,720
scanning is widely used with between 100

524
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:24,880
and 200 million such CT scans performed

525
00:20:22,720 --> 00:20:26,720
worldwide each year. This number

526
00:20:24,880 --> 00:20:29,440
highlights how prevalent and important

527
00:20:26,720 --> 00:20:31,360
this technology has become.

528
00:20:29,440 --> 00:20:33,120
Reflecting on that time, we didn't file

529
00:20:31,360 --> 00:20:35,600
any patents on this work, which turned

530
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:37,760
out to be a major missed opportunity.

531
00:20:35,600 --> 00:20:40,960
Had we filed for intellectual property,

532
00:20:37,760 --> 00:20:42,799
it could have been extremely valuable.

533
00:20:40,960 --> 00:20:44,960
Helical scanning combines multiple

534
00:20:42,799 --> 00:20:47,200
technologies, including slip ring

535
00:20:44,960 --> 00:20:49,840
transmission, X-ray sources, and

536
00:20:47,200 --> 00:20:51,840
sophisticated reconstruction algorithms.

537
00:20:49,840 --> 00:20:54,320
The software scanning model itself is

538
00:20:51,840 --> 00:20:56,400
crucial to performance.

539
00:20:54,320 --> 00:20:58,480
To put it in perspective, even if each

540
00:20:56,400 --> 00:21:00,720
scan were worth only one cent with a

541
00:20:58,480 --> 00:21:03,760
million scans annually, that's a million

542
00:21:00,720 --> 00:21:05,840
dollars a year in potential revenue.

543
00:21:03,760 --> 00:21:07,919
So, not securing patent protection was

544
00:21:05,840 --> 00:21:09,919
probably the biggest mistake we made.

545
00:21:07,919 --> 00:21:11,600
Nonetheless, our original paper still

546
00:21:09,919 --> 00:21:15,720
stands as the prototype of this

547
00:21:11,600 --> 00:21:15,720
important process and algorithm.

548
00:21:17,440 --> 00:21:21,919
Now, let's discuss longitudinal data

549
00:21:19,360 --> 00:21:24,080
truncation from a single slice. When you

550
00:21:21,919 --> 00:21:26,000
perform one helical turn with a single

551
00:21:24,080 --> 00:21:28,240
slice, you have a parallel beam and

552
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:30,400
adjacent rays allowing you to perform

553
00:21:28,240 --> 00:21:32,480
interpolation.

554
00:21:30,400 --> 00:21:34,640
However, when you move to a wider angle

555
00:21:32,480 --> 00:21:36,320
cone beam, simple interpolation is no

556
00:21:34,640 --> 00:21:38,880
longer sufficient and more advanced

557
00:21:36,320 --> 00:21:40,880
algorithms are needed. The first paper I

558
00:21:38,880 --> 00:21:43,039
wrote on cone beam hill scanning

559
00:21:40,880 --> 00:21:46,280
proposed an approximate algorithm for

560
00:21:43,039 --> 00:21:46,280
this challenge.

561
00:21:47,760 --> 00:21:51,600
It then took over a decade for the field

562
00:21:49,840 --> 00:21:54,320
to develop what is called the exact

563
00:21:51,600 --> 00:21:56,320
helical cone beam algorithm. This

564
00:21:54,320 --> 00:21:57,760
algorithm still uses the filtered back

565
00:21:56,320 --> 00:22:00,320
projection framework but is

566
00:21:57,760 --> 00:22:03,039
mathematically exact.

567
00:22:00,320 --> 00:22:04,960
The author Dr. Katzovich is a

568
00:22:03,039 --> 00:22:06,640
mathematician so the paper is quite

569
00:22:04,960 --> 00:22:09,039
dense and challenging for most

570
00:22:06,640 --> 00:22:11,360
engineering students. Nonetheless, it

571
00:22:09,039 --> 00:22:13,200
demonstrates that modern CT theory can

572
00:22:11,360 --> 00:22:15,600
effectively handle longitudinally

573
00:22:13,200 --> 00:22:17,440
truncated data.

574
00:22:15,600 --> 00:22:19,600
Earlier I mentioned that in the second

575
00:22:17,440 --> 00:22:22,400
generation data truncation happens

576
00:22:19,600 --> 00:22:24,640
transversely while helical CT mainly

577
00:22:22,400 --> 00:22:26,400
deals with longitudinal data truncation

578
00:22:24,640 --> 00:22:29,400
which can be seen as a symmetric

579
00:22:26,400 --> 00:22:29,400
problem.

580
00:22:30,880 --> 00:22:35,520
There is also an interesting transverse

581
00:22:32,720 --> 00:22:37,200
data truncation problem.

582
00:22:35,520 --> 00:22:39,360
Imagine your cross-section represents

583
00:22:37,200 --> 00:22:42,799
the green base while the blue area is a

584
00:22:39,360 --> 00:22:44,320
smaller region of interest or ROI.

585
00:22:42,799 --> 00:22:45,919
Perhaps you're only interested in the

586
00:22:44,320 --> 00:22:48,320
heart or in the ear. If planning a

587
00:22:45,919 --> 00:22:50,880
cclear implant,

588
00:22:48,320 --> 00:22:52,720
in this case, the ROI is small. You

589
00:22:50,880 --> 00:22:55,120
might use a narrow fan beam targeted

590
00:22:52,720 --> 00:22:58,000
only through the ROI, then rotate as in

591
00:22:55,120 --> 00:22:59,440
third generation fan beam geometry. This

592
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:02,720
means the projection data is

593
00:22:59,440 --> 00:23:05,200
transversely truncated on both sides.

594
00:23:02,720 --> 00:23:07,440
With truncation on both edges, the Fier

595
00:23:05,200 --> 00:23:10,720
slice theorem no longer applies, making

596
00:23:07,440 --> 00:23:13,440
reconstruction very challenging.

597
00:23:10,720 --> 00:23:16,240
Mathematically, this interior problem

598
00:23:13,440 --> 00:23:18,799
has no unique solution, meaning multiple

599
00:23:16,240 --> 00:23:21,919
structures could produce identical data,

600
00:23:18,799 --> 00:23:23,919
and you wouldn't know which is correct.

601
00:23:21,919 --> 00:23:25,919
Several years ago, my group revisited

602
00:23:23,919 --> 00:23:28,000
and solved this problem, which we felt

603
00:23:25,919 --> 00:23:29,919
very proud of.

604
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:32,159
While the classical interior problems

605
00:23:29,919 --> 00:23:33,919
non-uniqueness holds in unrestricted

606
00:23:32,159 --> 00:23:36,320
function spaces, we showed that

607
00:23:33,919 --> 00:23:39,320
introducing prior knowledge allows exact

608
00:23:36,320 --> 00:23:39,320
reconstruction.

609
00:23:39,440 --> 00:23:43,200
For example, if you know a small sub

610
00:23:41,200 --> 00:23:45,520
region of the ROI, such as air in the

611
00:23:43,200 --> 00:23:47,440
airway or blood in the aorta, you can

612
00:23:45,520 --> 00:23:50,400
use that known X-ray attenuation

613
00:23:47,440 --> 00:23:52,240
coefficient as a reference.

614
00:23:50,400 --> 00:23:54,320
This prior information enables

615
00:23:52,240 --> 00:23:57,200
theoretical exact reconstruction within

616
00:23:54,320 --> 00:24:00,080
the ROI from truncated data, a method

617
00:23:57,200 --> 00:24:01,919
called interior tomography. Our work was

618
00:24:00,080 --> 00:24:04,240
wellreceived, although reviewers raised

619
00:24:01,919 --> 00:24:06,480
important questions.

620
00:24:04,240 --> 00:24:08,640
One student tested whether blood density

621
00:24:06,480 --> 00:24:10,159
varies among different races or sexes,

622
00:24:08,640 --> 00:24:12,640
finding it consistent across

623
00:24:10,159 --> 00:24:14,720
populations. This allowed us to use

624
00:24:12,640 --> 00:24:16,720
blood density as a stable reference in

625
00:24:14,720 --> 00:24:21,440
cardiac imaging, so you only need to

626
00:24:16,720 --> 00:24:23,200
scan the heart, reducing radiation dose.

627
00:24:21,440 --> 00:24:25,520
Later, reviewers pointed out that

628
00:24:23,200 --> 00:24:28,000
contrast agents like iodine injected

629
00:24:25,520 --> 00:24:30,080
during CT angography change blood

630
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:32,159
density, so you can't always rely on a

631
00:24:30,080 --> 00:24:34,080
fixed reference.

632
00:24:32,159 --> 00:24:37,080
This challenge led us to develop a new

633
00:24:34,080 --> 00:24:37,080
solution.

634
00:24:38,640 --> 00:24:42,960
In the new approach, we assume there is

635
00:24:40,400 --> 00:24:44,960
no known sub region.

636
00:24:42,960 --> 00:24:47,200
Instead, we model the region of interest

637
00:24:44,960 --> 00:24:49,279
as composed of finitely many sub

638
00:24:47,200 --> 00:24:51,440
regions, each modeled by peace-wise

639
00:24:49,279 --> 00:24:53,440
polomials.

640
00:24:51,440 --> 00:24:56,159
The boundaries of these sub regions need

641
00:24:53,440 --> 00:24:58,080
not be known in advance. Each sub region

642
00:24:56,159 --> 00:25:00,559
can be approximated by constant

643
00:24:58,080 --> 00:25:02,640
quadratic or more general multivariable

644
00:25:00,559 --> 00:25:04,480
polomials.

645
00:25:02,640 --> 00:25:07,039
This assumption matches the idea of band

646
00:25:04,480 --> 00:25:09,200
limited signals from sampling theory.

647
00:25:07,039 --> 00:25:11,760
With this sparitybased model where the

648
00:25:09,200 --> 00:25:13,760
ROI is decomposed into a small number of

649
00:25:11,760 --> 00:25:18,400
smooth regions, the interior

650
00:25:13,760 --> 00:25:20,400
reconstruction problem becomes solvable.

651
00:25:18,400 --> 00:25:22,000
For example, in a chest CT scan, you can

652
00:25:20,400 --> 00:25:24,000
reconstruct the whole chest from all

653
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:26,480
data or just from truncated data

654
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:28,320
restricted to an ROI.

655
00:25:26,480 --> 00:25:31,360
Here is a real data set scanned on a

656
00:25:28,320 --> 00:25:33,200
seaman scanner demonstrating this.

657
00:25:31,360 --> 00:25:35,120
Interior tomography is no longer a

658
00:25:33,200 --> 00:25:39,720
mathematical curiosity, but delivers

659
00:25:35,120 --> 00:25:39,720
practically good reconstruction results.

660
00:25:41,200 --> 00:25:46,080
Let me add some comparison between

661
00:25:42,799 --> 00:25:48,000
classic CT and interior tomography.

662
00:25:46,080 --> 00:25:49,360
According to classical theory which we

663
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:51,440
learned and which I explained in

664
00:25:49,360 --> 00:25:53,440
textbooks, you need to collect all data

665
00:25:51,440 --> 00:25:55,520
to satisfy the data sufficiency

666
00:25:53,440 --> 00:25:56,880
condition. Usually a fan beam or cone

667
00:25:55,520 --> 00:25:59,520
beam that covers the entire

668
00:25:56,880 --> 00:26:01,600
cross-section or object.

669
00:25:59,520 --> 00:26:04,720
The data must comprehensively cover the

670
00:26:01,600 --> 00:26:06,640
object for accurate reconstruction.

671
00:26:04,720 --> 00:26:08,480
Interior tomography, however, only

672
00:26:06,640 --> 00:26:10,640
requires partial data limited to the

673
00:26:08,480 --> 00:26:12,559
region of interest.

674
00:26:10,640 --> 00:26:15,279
Thus, with filtered back projection,

675
00:26:12,559 --> 00:26:17,279
classic CT reconstructs a global image

676
00:26:15,279 --> 00:26:19,520
like a wholesale business covering the

677
00:26:17,279 --> 00:26:21,520
entire area.

678
00:26:19,520 --> 00:26:23,440
Interior tomography reconstructs a

679
00:26:21,520 --> 00:26:25,440
smaller targeted area like retail

680
00:26:23,440 --> 00:26:27,520
shopping. This opens up new

681
00:26:25,440 --> 00:26:29,279
opportunities, providing flexibility and

682
00:26:27,520 --> 00:26:31,200
precision.

683
00:26:29,279 --> 00:26:33,520
You only need to shoot X-rays through

684
00:26:31,200 --> 00:26:37,640
the important region, reducing dose and

685
00:26:33,520 --> 00:26:37,640
focusing on clinical needs.

686
00:26:39,039 --> 00:26:43,600
I have given multiple presentations on

687
00:26:41,039 --> 00:26:45,919
interior tomography. One key idea is

688
00:26:43,600 --> 00:26:48,799
that interior tomography uses less data.

689
00:26:45,919 --> 00:26:50,880
Less is more in many ways.

690
00:26:48,799 --> 00:26:53,520
Less data means a deeper understanding

691
00:26:50,880 --> 00:26:56,080
of imaging principles. We can handle

692
00:26:53,520 --> 00:26:58,880
larger objects, use less radiation dose,

693
00:26:56,080 --> 00:27:02,240
and achieve faster scanning.

694
00:26:58,880 --> 00:27:04,240
Why is it faster?

695
00:27:02,240 --> 00:27:06,080
All scanning geometries from first to

696
00:27:04,240 --> 00:27:08,480
helical generations aim to speed up

697
00:27:06,080 --> 00:27:10,799
scanning. A cone beam is better than a

698
00:27:08,480 --> 00:27:12,960
fan beam because it allows parallel data

699
00:27:10,799 --> 00:27:15,039
acquisition.

700
00:27:12,960 --> 00:27:16,880
Interior tomography takes this even

701
00:27:15,039 --> 00:27:19,120
further by enabling higher level

702
00:27:16,880 --> 00:27:22,159
parallelism with multiple X-ray sources

703
00:27:19,120 --> 00:27:23,760
and smaller detector arrays.

704
00:27:22,159 --> 00:27:26,240
In the extreme, you could have many

705
00:27:23,760 --> 00:27:28,240
X-ray focal spots possibly based on

706
00:27:26,240 --> 00:27:31,039
emerging technologies like carbon nano

707
00:27:28,240 --> 00:27:33,120
tube cold emission X-ray sources. These

708
00:27:31,039 --> 00:27:34,880
enable distributed X-ray cells with

709
00:27:33,120 --> 00:27:36,720
small detector elements arranged

710
00:27:34,880 --> 00:27:38,640
similarly to the fourth generation

711
00:27:36,720 --> 00:27:40,559
geometry.

712
00:27:38,640 --> 00:27:43,200
At any instance, such a system could

713
00:27:40,559 --> 00:27:45,200
collect multiple truncated projections.

714
00:27:43,200 --> 00:27:47,440
Handling data truncation like this

715
00:27:45,200 --> 00:27:49,279
allows reconstruction focused on small

716
00:27:47,440 --> 00:27:51,600
regions of interest achieving the

717
00:27:49,279 --> 00:27:54,080
fastest possible tomographic imaging

718
00:27:51,600 --> 00:27:56,159
speed. You cannot go faster than the

719
00:27:54,080 --> 00:27:58,399
speed of light which governs X-ray

720
00:27:56,159 --> 00:28:00,399
photon travel.

721
00:27:58,399 --> 00:28:03,039
We filed for intellectual property on

722
00:28:00,399 --> 00:28:05,039
this concept at Virginia Tech. Later I

723
00:28:03,039 --> 00:28:09,399
will discuss the potential of interior

724
00:28:05,039 --> 00:28:09,399
tomography in cardiac imaging.

725
00:28:10,880 --> 00:28:14,960
Now let me explain CT scanner

726
00:28:12,559 --> 00:28:16,880
architecture. To reconstruct images, we

727
00:28:14,960 --> 00:28:18,559
rely on line integrals from X-ray

728
00:28:16,880 --> 00:28:20,720
projections using filtered back

729
00:28:18,559 --> 00:28:23,120
projection and forier transformbased

730
00:28:20,720 --> 00:28:26,080
reconstruction or interior tomography

731
00:28:23,120 --> 00:28:28,080
for region of interest reconstruction.

732
00:28:26,080 --> 00:28:30,559
To acquire this data, you need hardware,

733
00:28:28,080 --> 00:28:31,919
the CT scanner itself. This is a

734
00:28:30,559 --> 00:28:33,679
donut-shaped structure called the

735
00:28:31,919 --> 00:28:35,919
gantry, which is the framework holding

736
00:28:33,679 --> 00:28:37,919
all components.

737
00:28:35,919 --> 00:28:39,520
Inside, you find X-ray sources and

738
00:28:37,919 --> 00:28:42,399
columators that shape the beam and

739
00:28:39,520 --> 00:28:44,480
filter out low energy X-rays. Low energy

740
00:28:42,399 --> 00:28:47,520
photons won't penetrate the patient and

741
00:28:44,480 --> 00:28:49,200
only add an unwanted dose.

742
00:28:47,520 --> 00:28:51,039
Columators also reject scattered

743
00:28:49,200 --> 00:28:52,960
radiation to ensure the measured signal

744
00:28:51,039 --> 00:28:54,880
corresponds closely to the direct line

745
00:28:52,960 --> 00:28:57,279
integrals.

746
00:28:54,880 --> 00:28:59,279
The scanner is a sophisticated device.

747
00:28:57,279 --> 00:29:01,840
You place the patient in the gantry and

748
00:28:59,279 --> 00:29:06,440
from that you acquire many images that

749
00:29:01,840 --> 00:29:06,440
resolve internal structures clearly.

750
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:12,320
Let me show you a typical CT scanner

751
00:29:10,080 --> 00:29:13,919
layout. You have the X-ray tube which is

752
00:29:12,320 --> 00:29:16,880
quite heavy. For example, our lab's

753
00:29:13,919 --> 00:29:18,640
donated GE tube is heavy.

754
00:29:16,880 --> 00:29:20,880
Powering the tube requires a high

755
00:29:18,640 --> 00:29:23,200
voltage supply and a cooling system.

756
00:29:20,880 --> 00:29:24,799
Scanning consumes energy and heats the

757
00:29:23,200 --> 00:29:26,880
tube.

758
00:29:24,799 --> 00:29:28,720
Cooling is done with water or air to

759
00:29:26,880 --> 00:29:30,640
keep components at safe operating

760
00:29:28,720 --> 00:29:33,120
temperatures.

761
00:29:30,640 --> 00:29:35,039
This entire setup is called the gantry.

762
00:29:33,120 --> 00:29:36,960
It includes the patient table where the

763
00:29:35,039 --> 00:29:39,360
patient lies, the detector array

764
00:29:36,960 --> 00:29:41,600
opposite the source and columators in

765
00:29:39,360 --> 00:29:44,159
front of the detectors.

766
00:29:41,600 --> 00:29:46,080
In conventional CT, the source detectors

767
00:29:44,159 --> 00:29:48,559
and associated hardware are mounted on

768
00:29:46,080 --> 00:29:50,640
the rotating gantry.

769
00:29:48,559 --> 00:29:52,799
During scanning, the gantry physically

770
00:29:50,640 --> 00:29:55,200
rotates, typically half or a full

771
00:29:52,799 --> 00:29:57,039
circle, then reverses direction. This

772
00:29:55,200 --> 00:29:58,960
back and forth rotation is limited by

773
00:29:57,039 --> 00:30:00,559
cabling, which can tangle with repeated

774
00:29:58,960 --> 00:30:02,320
turns.

775
00:30:00,559 --> 00:30:04,559
For helical scanning, slip ring

776
00:30:02,320 --> 00:30:06,480
technology is employed. The slip ring

777
00:30:04,559 --> 00:30:08,960
lets you rotate continuously without

778
00:30:06,480 --> 00:30:10,640
cables twisting or breaking. It consists

779
00:30:08,960 --> 00:30:12,799
of rings with carefully designed

780
00:30:10,640 --> 00:30:16,320
contacts to transmit power to the X-ray

781
00:30:12,799 --> 00:30:17,919
source and data from the detectors. Slip

782
00:30:16,320 --> 00:30:20,480
ring technology is critical for

783
00:30:17,919 --> 00:30:22,320
continuous and fast scanning. It enables

784
00:30:20,480 --> 00:30:24,080
the transfer of data to the scanner's

785
00:30:22,320 --> 00:30:26,000
computer or local storage during

786
00:30:24,080 --> 00:30:28,240
rotation.

787
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:30,559
When designing a CT scanner, the goal is

788
00:30:28,240 --> 00:30:32,799
faster and faster imaging speed with the

789
00:30:30,559 --> 00:30:37,320
most demanding application being cardiac

790
00:30:32,799 --> 00:30:37,320
imaging, the holy grail of CT.

791
00:30:39,039 --> 00:30:43,679
At RPI, we have collaborated with GE

792
00:30:41,360 --> 00:30:45,520
Global Research Center and with Bruno DM

793
00:30:43,679 --> 00:30:48,159
and others to develop optimized

794
00:30:45,520 --> 00:30:50,000
architectures for dedicated cardiac CT

795
00:30:48,159 --> 00:30:51,760
scanners.

796
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:53,360
We published a paper last year on this

797
00:30:51,760 --> 00:30:55,200
topic.

798
00:30:53,360 --> 00:30:56,880
Designing a CT scanner is much like

799
00:30:55,200 --> 00:31:01,640
designing a building or room you

800
00:30:56,880 --> 00:31:01,640
consider architecture an optimal layout.

801
00:31:03,039 --> 00:31:07,679
I am especially excited about this

802
00:31:04,720 --> 00:31:10,159
project collaborating with GE.

803
00:31:07,679 --> 00:31:12,480
Currently cardiac CT scanners mostly use

804
00:31:10,159 --> 00:31:14,720
third generation geometry but with cone

805
00:31:12,480 --> 00:31:16,640
beam helical scanning mode which some

806
00:31:14,720 --> 00:31:19,679
call improved third generation or

807
00:31:16,640 --> 00:31:21,760
helical cone beam generation.

808
00:31:19,679 --> 00:31:23,520
This system is already very fast capable

809
00:31:21,760 --> 00:31:25,200
of rotating three to four times per

810
00:31:23,520 --> 00:31:27,440
second.

811
00:31:25,200 --> 00:31:29,600
The mechanical acceleration involved is

812
00:31:27,440 --> 00:31:32,640
astonishing much higher than what's

813
00:31:29,600 --> 00:31:34,720
involved in launching a space shuttle.

814
00:31:32,640 --> 00:31:37,120
The current best temporal and spatial

815
00:31:34,720 --> 00:31:39,039
resolution for cardiac CT is about 500

816
00:31:37,120 --> 00:31:41,279
microns.

817
00:31:39,039 --> 00:31:43,760
But according to cardiac surgeons I know

818
00:31:41,279 --> 00:31:46,240
100 micron resolution would solve many

819
00:31:43,760 --> 00:31:48,799
clinical challenges.

820
00:31:46,240 --> 00:31:50,559
Improving cardiac CT resolution requires

821
00:31:48,799 --> 00:31:54,080
higher imaging speed to freeze the

822
00:31:50,559 --> 00:31:56,080
heart's rapid motion on this fine scale.

823
00:31:54,080 --> 00:31:58,159
However, as you increase rotation speed,

824
00:31:56,080 --> 00:32:00,159
the X-ray tube has less time to emit

825
00:31:58,159 --> 00:32:02,480
photons, limiting achievable image

826
00:32:00,159 --> 00:32:05,279
quality. Our out of the box solution

827
00:32:02,480 --> 00:32:07,360
uses interior tomography. We designed a

828
00:32:05,279 --> 00:32:10,399
stationary ring scanner to boost X-ray

829
00:32:07,360 --> 00:32:12,399
flux by orders of magnitude.

830
00:32:10,399 --> 00:32:14,559
Imagine a cardiac patient lying still

831
00:32:12,399 --> 00:32:17,039
while being scanned. You get immediate

832
00:32:14,559 --> 00:32:19,120
images. When higher resolution is

833
00:32:17,039 --> 00:32:21,200
needed, the patient or ring can be

834
00:32:19,120 --> 00:32:23,440
translated to focus precisely on the

835
00:32:21,200 --> 00:32:25,519
region of interest.

836
00:32:23,440 --> 00:32:27,519
This stationary ring can move up, down,

837
00:32:25,519 --> 00:32:30,720
left, or right to align perfectly with

838
00:32:27,519 --> 00:32:32,720
conventional cardiac CT images.

839
00:32:30,720 --> 00:32:34,720
I believe this design is a promising way

840
00:32:32,720 --> 00:32:36,799
forward. An out-of-the-box solution for

841
00:32:34,720 --> 00:32:39,799
high-speed highresolution cardiac

842
00:32:36,799 --> 00:32:39,799
imaging.

843
00:32:41,360 --> 00:32:46,000
Now, let's finish with the last two

844
00:32:43,039 --> 00:32:47,840
sections. First, image artifacts. I've

845
00:32:46,000 --> 00:32:50,080
mentioned image artifacts several times

846
00:32:47,840 --> 00:32:51,679
throughout this course. Tomographic

847
00:32:50,080 --> 00:32:54,720
images are meant to reveal the true

848
00:32:51,679 --> 00:32:56,720
underlying anatomical structure.

849
00:32:54,720 --> 00:32:58,559
However, for various reasons, the images

850
00:32:56,720 --> 00:33:00,880
sometimes show structures that aren't

851
00:32:58,559 --> 00:33:03,919
actually present. These false structures

852
00:33:00,880 --> 00:33:05,760
are what we call image artifacts.

853
00:33:03,919 --> 00:33:07,919
If there's no real structure present,

854
00:33:05,760 --> 00:33:10,080
what you see might just be noise.

855
00:33:07,919 --> 00:33:12,000
Sometimes artifacts show up as streaks

856
00:33:10,080 --> 00:33:13,840
or stripes in the image. But obviously,

857
00:33:12,000 --> 00:33:17,120
there are no stripes inside the human

858
00:33:13,840 --> 00:33:19,120
body. They are just artifacts.

859
00:33:17,120 --> 00:33:21,519
We'll discuss CT image quality

860
00:33:19,120 --> 00:33:23,600
considerations next, which include how

861
00:33:21,519 --> 00:33:26,600
artifacts impact the overall image

862
00:33:23,600 --> 00:33:26,600
quality.

863
00:33:28,320 --> 00:33:32,640
Starting with data sampling. In modern

864
00:33:30,399 --> 00:33:34,559
CT scanners, such as those from GE or

865
00:33:32,640 --> 00:33:36,559
Seammens, a typical rotation collects

866
00:33:34,559 --> 00:33:38,000
about 1,000 projections, sometimes

867
00:33:36,559 --> 00:33:42,080
slightly fewer depending on the

868
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:44,159
manufacturer, like 800 or 790.

869
00:33:42,080 --> 00:33:46,240
At each projection, the detector array

870
00:33:44,159 --> 00:33:48,320
works like a comb beam or multirow

871
00:33:46,240 --> 00:33:50,720
detector, capturing thousands of data

872
00:33:48,320 --> 00:33:52,320
points along each ray path. For example,

873
00:33:50,720 --> 00:33:55,519
along one ray direction, you might have

874
00:33:52,320 --> 00:33:57,279
about 1,000 detector elements.

875
00:33:55,519 --> 00:33:59,200
All these detector readings combine to

876
00:33:57,279 --> 00:34:00,799
define the scanner's spatial resolution,

877
00:33:59,200 --> 00:34:02,559
often achieving sub millimeter

878
00:34:00,799 --> 00:34:04,640
precision.

879
00:34:02,559 --> 00:34:06,880
In the future, we are aiming to improve

880
00:34:04,640 --> 00:34:10,079
this further, reaching resolutions on

881
00:34:06,880 --> 00:34:11,839
the order of 100 microns.

882
00:34:10,079 --> 00:34:14,159
This is challenging because every wire

883
00:34:11,839 --> 00:34:16,000
and detector element has limits. So

884
00:34:14,159 --> 00:34:19,280
current strategies often focus on

885
00:34:16,000 --> 00:34:21,520
targeting smaller regions of interest.

886
00:34:19,280 --> 00:34:23,599
To resolve fine details, the angular

887
00:34:21,520 --> 00:34:25,839
sampling intervals and detector element

888
00:34:23,599 --> 00:34:27,919
sizes must be small enough consistent

889
00:34:25,839 --> 00:34:29,520
with forier analysis and sampling

890
00:34:27,919 --> 00:34:31,599
theory.

891
00:34:29,520 --> 00:34:33,760
If the sampling rate is too low or the

892
00:34:31,599 --> 00:34:36,879
detector elements are too big, small

893
00:34:33,760 --> 00:34:39,280
features won't be resolved clearly.

894
00:34:36,879 --> 00:34:41,440
For instance, with about 512 rays, you

895
00:34:39,280 --> 00:34:43,679
get fairly clear images. With fewer

896
00:34:41,440 --> 00:34:45,599
rays, small details become difficult to

897
00:34:43,679 --> 00:34:48,000
detect.

898
00:34:45,599 --> 00:34:49,919
Angular sampling is also critical. If

899
00:34:48,000 --> 00:34:52,399
you have nearly 1,000 views around the

900
00:34:49,919 --> 00:34:54,399
object, image quality is good. Using

901
00:34:52,399 --> 00:34:58,200
fewer views leads to aliasing artifacts

902
00:34:54,399 --> 00:34:58,200
and lower image quality.

903
00:34:59,839 --> 00:35:04,320
CT reconstruction relies on CT numbers,

904
00:35:02,320 --> 00:35:06,079
which are derived from Beer's law.

905
00:35:04,320 --> 00:35:08,320
Beer's law relates the incoming and

906
00:35:06,079 --> 00:35:10,480
attenuated X-ray intensities via a

907
00:35:08,320 --> 00:35:12,720
parameter called the linear attenuation

908
00:35:10,480 --> 00:35:14,400
coefficient mu.

909
00:35:12,720 --> 00:35:16,240
Data processing converts raw

910
00:35:14,400 --> 00:35:18,480
measurements into line integrals which

911
00:35:16,240 --> 00:35:20,720
are then used to reconstruct mu values

912
00:35:18,480 --> 00:35:22,960
throughout the image.

913
00:35:20,720 --> 00:35:24,880
On the CT monitor, you don't see mu

914
00:35:22,960 --> 00:35:26,960
values directly but relative values

915
00:35:24,880 --> 00:35:29,200
compared to water since the human body

916
00:35:26,960 --> 00:35:31,119
is mostly water.

917
00:35:29,200 --> 00:35:33,200
Thus, Connecticut numbers indicate how

918
00:35:31,119 --> 00:35:35,839
much more or less dense a region is than

919
00:35:33,200 --> 00:35:38,160
water.

920
00:35:35,839 --> 00:35:40,720
For example, bone has a CT number over

921
00:35:38,160 --> 00:35:43,200
1,000, air has about minus 1,000, and

922
00:35:40,720 --> 00:35:45,680
water is defined as zero. The range is

923
00:35:43,200 --> 00:35:47,839
often magnified by 1,000 times for

924
00:35:45,680 --> 00:35:49,680
visualization.

925
00:35:47,839 --> 00:35:51,680
This scale is sometimes called

926
00:35:49,680 --> 00:35:53,200
Houndsfield Units, named after Sir

927
00:35:51,680 --> 00:35:55,119
Godfrey Houndsfield, the British

928
00:35:53,200 --> 00:35:56,880
engineer who developed the first CT

929
00:35:55,119 --> 00:35:58,960
scanner, which earned him a Nobel Prize

930
00:35:56,880 --> 00:36:02,839
for its revolutionary capability to

931
00:35:58,960 --> 00:36:02,839
reveal internal structures.

932
00:36:04,400 --> 00:36:08,400
Different body regions use different CT

933
00:36:06,320 --> 00:36:10,320
number windows for display. These

934
00:36:08,400 --> 00:36:12,480
windows are defined by minimum and

935
00:36:10,320 --> 00:36:15,760
maximum CT numbers depending on the

936
00:36:12,480 --> 00:36:17,599
manufacturer or clinical application.

937
00:36:15,760 --> 00:36:20,880
For instance, the full range of CT

938
00:36:17,599 --> 00:36:24,320
numbers might be mapped to 256 grayscale

939
00:36:20,880 --> 00:36:26,240
levels on a display from white to black.

940
00:36:24,320 --> 00:36:29,280
The human eye can typically distinguish

941
00:36:26,240 --> 00:36:30,960
about 50 shades within a window. If too

942
00:36:29,280 --> 00:36:33,920
many shades are presented, subtle

943
00:36:30,960 --> 00:36:36,079
differences become difficult to see.

944
00:36:33,920 --> 00:36:38,560
For lung imaging, for example, you might

945
00:36:36,079 --> 00:36:40,720
set the window maximum to about 250

946
00:36:38,560 --> 00:36:44,480
houndsfield units and the minimum to the

947
00:36:40,720 --> 00:36:46,880
air value. Within this window, 256 gray

948
00:36:44,480 --> 00:36:48,480
levels help render tissues clearly.

949
00:36:46,880 --> 00:36:51,119
Anything outside the window appears

950
00:36:48,480 --> 00:36:53,119
clipped and is not visible.

951
00:36:51,119 --> 00:36:54,880
This windowing is critical to tailoring

952
00:36:53,119 --> 00:36:57,880
visualization to different clinical

953
00:36:54,880 --> 00:36:57,880
needs.

954
00:36:59,440 --> 00:37:04,400
There are multiple resolution measures

955
00:37:01,280 --> 00:37:06,640
in CT. High contrast resolution detects

956
00:37:04,400 --> 00:37:09,520
small objects adjacently positioned. For

957
00:37:06,640 --> 00:37:12,480
example, cardiac CT currently resolves

958
00:37:09,520 --> 00:37:14,480
about 500 microns.

959
00:37:12,480 --> 00:37:17,440
If you want to detect even smaller

960
00:37:14,480 --> 00:37:19,440
details down to 100 microns, you may

961
00:37:17,440 --> 00:37:20,880
need advanced methods like interior

962
00:37:19,440 --> 00:37:23,040
tomography that we've discussed

963
00:37:20,880 --> 00:37:25,040
extensively.

964
00:37:23,040 --> 00:37:27,440
Low contrast resolution differentiates

965
00:37:25,040 --> 00:37:29,280
subtle intensity differences. This is

966
00:37:27,440 --> 00:37:32,160
important because tumors may look very

967
00:37:29,280 --> 00:37:34,320
similar to surrounding tissue.

968
00:37:32,160 --> 00:37:36,640
Narrow window settings chosen carefully

969
00:37:34,320 --> 00:37:39,440
with a mean and range help highlight

970
00:37:36,640 --> 00:37:41,280
these subtle differences. If the CT

971
00:37:39,440 --> 00:37:43,680
number difference is very small, say 5

972
00:37:41,280 --> 00:37:45,760
to 10 houndsfield units and noise is

973
00:37:43,680 --> 00:37:50,320
comparable, detecting such differences

974
00:37:45,760 --> 00:37:52,640
requires boosting contrast effectively.

975
00:37:50,320 --> 00:37:54,800
Hence, low contrast resolution depends

976
00:37:52,640 --> 00:37:57,520
not just on hardware but also on image

977
00:37:54,800 --> 00:37:59,359
noise characteristics.

978
00:37:57,520 --> 00:38:01,599
Temporal resolution is crucial for

979
00:37:59,359 --> 00:38:03,680
dynamic organs like the heart. To get

980
00:38:01,599 --> 00:38:05,680
high resolution cardiac images, both

981
00:38:03,680 --> 00:38:08,320
spatial and temporal resolution must be

982
00:38:05,680 --> 00:38:10,160
adequate. If your detector resolves fine

983
00:38:08,320 --> 00:38:12,079
details but scanning speed is too slow,

984
00:38:10,160 --> 00:38:14,560
motion blurring will reduce effective

985
00:38:12,079 --> 00:38:16,720
spatial resolution.

986
00:38:14,560 --> 00:38:19,520
So achieving both fast scanning and high

987
00:38:16,720 --> 00:38:21,200
spatial resolution is essential. Photon

988
00:38:19,520 --> 00:38:23,520
counting detectors with elements as

989
00:38:21,200 --> 00:38:26,720
small as 55 microns are available

990
00:38:23,520 --> 00:38:28,800
enabling very high resolution imaging.

991
00:38:26,720 --> 00:38:30,640
Combining this with interior tomography

992
00:38:28,800 --> 00:38:32,640
concepts and distributed thousands of

993
00:38:30,640 --> 00:38:34,800
photon counting detector elements, it's

994
00:38:32,640 --> 00:38:36,800
feasible to build a magic ring scanner

995
00:38:34,800 --> 00:38:39,599
that focuses on small regions of

996
00:38:36,800 --> 00:38:41,680
interest with resolution down to 100

997
00:38:39,599 --> 00:38:43,520
microns.

998
00:38:41,680 --> 00:38:47,320
This is an area of active research that

999
00:38:43,520 --> 00:38:47,320
we continue to pursue.

1000
00:38:48,880 --> 00:38:52,720
The noise you see in CT images is due to

1001
00:38:51,040 --> 00:38:55,520
quantum effects and other random

1002
00:38:52,720 --> 00:38:57,280
variations. Several factors influence

1003
00:38:55,520 --> 00:39:00,480
this noise level, such as the tube

1004
00:38:57,280 --> 00:39:02,400
current, often referred to as mass.

1005
00:39:00,480 --> 00:39:04,160
When the tube current is high, the X-ray

1006
00:39:02,400 --> 00:39:06,320
tube emits more photons, so the

1007
00:39:04,160 --> 00:39:08,800
statistical fluctuations decrease, which

1008
00:39:06,320 --> 00:39:11,280
means less noise.

1009
00:39:08,800 --> 00:39:13,760
Image resolution also plays a key role.

1010
00:39:11,280 --> 00:39:16,000
Think of pixel or voxil size in a simple

1011
00:39:13,760 --> 00:39:18,000
way. If you have a large pixel, it

1012
00:39:16,000 --> 00:39:20,160
collects many photons resulting in a

1013
00:39:18,000 --> 00:39:21,839
lower noise level.

1014
00:39:20,160 --> 00:39:24,720
But if you target a very small

1015
00:39:21,839 --> 00:39:26,880
highresolution pixel or voxil, fewer

1016
00:39:24,720 --> 00:39:29,119
photons pass through it. So you need

1017
00:39:26,880 --> 00:39:31,520
even more photons overall to maintain

1018
00:39:29,119 --> 00:39:33,680
good image quality.

1019
00:39:31,520 --> 00:39:36,320
This is why interior tomography is

1020
00:39:33,680 --> 00:39:37,920
advantageous. Since it directs photons

1021
00:39:36,320 --> 00:39:40,160
only through the small region of

1022
00:39:37,920 --> 00:39:42,560
interest, it reduces unnecessary

1023
00:39:40,160 --> 00:39:46,359
exposure while maintaining image quality

1024
00:39:42,560 --> 00:39:46,359
for the target structure.

1025
00:39:48,000 --> 00:39:51,839
Low contrast image resolution can be

1026
00:39:50,000 --> 00:39:53,520
measured using resolution phantoms

1027
00:39:51,839 --> 00:39:55,839
containing small test objects with

1028
00:39:53,520 --> 00:39:57,680
subtle differences in density. These

1029
00:39:55,839 --> 00:39:59,359
phantoms help to assess if the scanner

1030
00:39:57,680 --> 00:40:01,280
and reconstruction algorithm can

1031
00:39:59,359 --> 00:40:03,200
visualize objects with minor contrast

1032
00:40:01,280 --> 00:40:04,960
differences.

1033
00:40:03,200 --> 00:40:06,800
When contrast is poor, like between

1034
00:40:04,960 --> 00:40:08,880
blood vessels and surrounding tissue,

1035
00:40:06,800 --> 00:40:11,280
contrast agents such as iodine are

1036
00:40:08,880 --> 00:40:13,280
injected. These agents highlight the

1037
00:40:11,280 --> 00:40:16,160
vessels, making blood, tumors, and

1038
00:40:13,280 --> 00:40:18,400
related structures more visible.

1039
00:40:16,160 --> 00:40:20,480
This approach significantly improves low

1040
00:40:18,400 --> 00:40:22,480
contrast resolution enabling better

1041
00:40:20,480 --> 00:40:25,480
diagnosis and visualization of soft

1042
00:40:22,480 --> 00:40:25,480
tissues.

1043
00:40:27,040 --> 00:40:31,119
Temporal resolution. So you see

1044
00:40:28,640 --> 00:40:34,160
conventional CP scanning. You do one

1045
00:40:31,119 --> 00:40:36,160
slice at a time. Then you

1046
00:40:34,160 --> 00:40:38,640
move the patient into the country by a

1047
00:40:36,160 --> 00:40:40,320
small increment. Do again patient. Keep

1048
00:40:38,640 --> 00:40:42,160
pressing.

1049
00:40:40,320 --> 00:40:44,400
So from slice to slice you see some

1050
00:40:42,160 --> 00:40:47,040
discontinuity. So this is not due to

1051
00:40:44,400 --> 00:40:48,960
some pathological feature.

1052
00:40:47,040 --> 00:40:50,880
It's really due to motion artifacts that

1053
00:40:48,960 --> 00:40:54,000
show the temporal resolution agent is

1054
00:40:50,880 --> 00:40:55,680
good enough. But with helical scanning,

1055
00:40:54,000 --> 00:40:59,280
it's smoothed out, but you have some

1056
00:40:55,680 --> 00:41:02,079
longitudinal blurring.

1057
00:40:59,280 --> 00:41:04,480
Here is the earlier algorithm. Later I

1058
00:41:02,079 --> 00:41:06,560
mention overlapping reconstruction. You

1059
00:41:04,480 --> 00:41:08,560
really can recover an image

1060
00:41:06,560 --> 00:41:11,680
longitudinally and a better resolution

1061
00:41:08,560 --> 00:41:13,040
than conventional scanning. Anyway, so

1062
00:41:11,680 --> 00:41:16,040
we wouldn't have time to go into

1063
00:41:13,040 --> 00:41:16,040
details.

1064
00:41:17,839 --> 00:41:22,160
Scattering artifacts occur due to

1065
00:41:19,680 --> 00:41:24,560
scattered X-ray photons. Let me explain

1066
00:41:22,160 --> 00:41:26,160
this with an example. Imagine sending an

1067
00:41:24,560 --> 00:41:28,160
X-ray beam from the south direction

1068
00:41:26,160 --> 00:41:30,560
through the body.

1069
00:41:28,160 --> 00:41:32,480
Follow my cursor as I illustrate. The

1070
00:41:30,560 --> 00:41:34,319
primary X-ray beam travels straight

1071
00:41:32,480 --> 00:41:35,920
through a data element, creating the

1072
00:41:34,319 --> 00:41:37,920
expected line integral for

1073
00:41:35,920 --> 00:41:40,160
reconstruction.

1074
00:41:37,920 --> 00:41:42,640
However, during measurement, some X-rays

1075
00:41:40,160 --> 00:41:44,560
scatter in various directions. You might

1076
00:41:42,640 --> 00:41:46,800
see scattering toward the right, left,

1077
00:41:44,560 --> 00:41:48,400
or even backward directions. These

1078
00:41:46,800 --> 00:41:50,240
scattered photons add a background

1079
00:41:48,400 --> 00:41:53,440
signal that does not correspond to the

1080
00:41:50,240 --> 00:41:55,440
direct transmission along the beam path.

1081
00:41:53,440 --> 00:41:57,599
Because of this scattered radiation, the

1082
00:41:55,440 --> 00:42:00,319
detector picks up additional signals on

1083
00:41:57,599 --> 00:42:02,319
top of the real primary X-rays. This

1084
00:42:00,319 --> 00:42:04,160
extra background leads to artifacts in

1085
00:42:02,319 --> 00:42:06,720
the image, usually appearing as low

1086
00:42:04,160 --> 00:42:09,359
frequency streaking or haze that reduces

1087
00:42:06,720 --> 00:42:11,280
contrast and clarity.

1088
00:42:09,359 --> 00:42:13,680
If you correct for scattering components

1089
00:42:11,280 --> 00:42:15,680
by estimating and subtracting them, you

1090
00:42:13,680 --> 00:42:17,520
can restore the image quality close to

1091
00:42:15,680 --> 00:42:19,280
the phantom reconstruction without

1092
00:42:17,520 --> 00:42:21,359
scatter.

1093
00:42:19,280 --> 00:42:22,960
One common method to reduce scatter is

1094
00:42:21,359 --> 00:42:25,520
using beam blockers placed

1095
00:42:22,960 --> 00:42:27,359
strategically. These beam blockers

1096
00:42:25,520 --> 00:42:29,920
function to block certain parts of the

1097
00:42:27,359 --> 00:42:32,160
primary radiation, allowing measurement

1098
00:42:29,920 --> 00:42:35,160
of scatter separately and improved image

1099
00:42:32,160 --> 00:42:35,160
correction.

1100
00:42:36,800 --> 00:42:41,599
Beam hardening artifacts occur when

1101
00:42:39,200 --> 00:42:43,760
X-ray beams pass through an object,

1102
00:42:41,599 --> 00:42:46,720
resulting in changes in beam energy

1103
00:42:43,760 --> 00:42:49,200
distribution that affect image quality.

1104
00:42:46,720 --> 00:42:52,000
Let me explain this in some detail.

1105
00:42:49,200 --> 00:42:54,000
Imagine an X-ray beam going through the

1106
00:42:52,000 --> 00:42:56,640
body in a certain direction forming a

1107
00:42:54,000 --> 00:42:58,800
parallel beam projection. This is a real

1108
00:42:56,640 --> 00:43:01,040
and common setup.

1109
00:42:58,800 --> 00:43:03,520
When using a medical X-ray tube and

1110
00:43:01,040 --> 00:43:06,000
current detector technology, both

1111
00:43:03,520 --> 00:43:09,440
measure the attenuated signals resulting

1112
00:43:06,000 --> 00:43:11,520
in what we call the line integral.

1113
00:43:09,440 --> 00:43:13,520
However, if you reconstruct the image

1114
00:43:11,520 --> 00:43:15,760
based on the uncorrected projection

1115
00:43:13,520 --> 00:43:18,319
profile, the reconstruction may look

1116
00:43:15,760 --> 00:43:20,960
distorted, often represented by a dotted

1117
00:43:18,319 --> 00:43:23,839
projection line in diagrams. Why does

1118
00:43:20,960 --> 00:43:26,480
this happen?

1119
00:43:23,839 --> 00:43:28,480
It's due to beam hardening. X-ray beams

1120
00:43:26,480 --> 00:43:30,560
are polychromatic, meaning they contain

1121
00:43:28,480 --> 00:43:32,319
photons of various energies forming a

1122
00:43:30,560 --> 00:43:34,640
spectrum.

1123
00:43:32,319 --> 00:43:36,960
Initially, the beam may have many lower

1124
00:43:34,640 --> 00:43:40,160
energy photons that get attenuated more

1125
00:43:36,960 --> 00:43:42,160
as the beam penetrates the object.

1126
00:43:40,160 --> 00:43:44,240
Lower energy photons have higher

1127
00:43:42,160 --> 00:43:47,119
attenuation coefficients, so they are

1128
00:43:44,240 --> 00:43:48,880
absorbed more readily in tissue.

1129
00:43:47,119 --> 00:43:50,560
As the beam progresses into the body,

1130
00:43:48,880 --> 00:43:52,720
the relative amount of higher energy

1131
00:43:50,560 --> 00:43:55,520
photons increases because they penetrate

1132
00:43:52,720 --> 00:43:58,240
deeper. This process hardens the beam,

1133
00:43:55,520 --> 00:44:00,240
increasing its average energy.

1134
00:43:58,240 --> 00:44:02,079
Since higher energy photons are less

1135
00:44:00,240 --> 00:44:04,880
easily blocked, the beam becomes more

1136
00:44:02,079 --> 00:44:06,800
penetrating along longer paths. Despite

1137
00:44:04,880 --> 00:44:09,040
this, many reconstruction algorithms

1138
00:44:06,800 --> 00:44:11,680
assume a monochromatic beam, leading to

1139
00:44:09,040 --> 00:44:13,760
errors.

1140
00:44:11,680 --> 00:44:16,160
This results in an underestimation of

1141
00:44:13,760 --> 00:44:17,839
the linear attenuation coefficient mu

1142
00:44:16,160 --> 00:44:20,319
because the beam appears to pass more

1143
00:44:17,839 --> 00:44:22,160
easily than expected.

1144
00:44:20,319 --> 00:44:24,319
Consequently, the computed line

1145
00:44:22,160 --> 00:44:26,880
integrals and pre-processed data are

1146
00:44:24,319 --> 00:44:29,359
based on a single energy assumption, but

1147
00:44:26,880 --> 00:44:31,119
the reality is a changing polychromatic

1148
00:44:29,359 --> 00:44:33,119
beam.

1149
00:44:31,119 --> 00:44:35,200
This discrepancy causes characteristic

1150
00:44:33,119 --> 00:44:37,680
artifacts in images such as streaks and

1151
00:44:35,200 --> 00:44:39,520
cupping. Proper correction methods and

1152
00:44:37,680 --> 00:44:41,680
models that account for beam hardening

1153
00:44:39,520 --> 00:44:45,720
are necessary to reduce these artifacts

1154
00:44:41,680 --> 00:44:45,720
and improve image quality.

1155
00:44:47,440 --> 00:44:51,520
This is an example of CT images affected

1156
00:44:49,920 --> 00:44:53,839
by beam hardening without any

1157
00:44:51,520 --> 00:44:57,359
correction. You can see a darker strip

1158
00:44:53,839 --> 00:44:59,040
in the image which obscures details.

1159
00:44:57,359 --> 00:45:00,880
After applying correction techniques,

1160
00:44:59,040 --> 00:45:03,359
you can visualize structures like gray

1161
00:45:00,880 --> 00:45:05,280
matter much more clearly.

1162
00:45:03,359 --> 00:45:07,280
Beam hardening artifacts originate from

1163
00:45:05,280 --> 00:45:10,000
the X-ray source and the polychromatic

1164
00:45:07,280 --> 00:45:12,160
nature of the X-ray beam. When you use a

1165
00:45:10,000 --> 00:45:14,400
photon counting detector, it can measure

1166
00:45:12,160 --> 00:45:16,240
X-ray signals at different energy levels

1167
00:45:14,400 --> 00:45:18,079
separately

1168
00:45:16,240 --> 00:45:20,160
by distinguishing between energies.

1169
00:45:18,079 --> 00:45:23,040
Photon counting detectors effectively

1170
00:45:20,160 --> 00:45:25,040
eliminate beam hardening artifacts. This

1171
00:45:23,040 --> 00:45:27,119
technology significantly improves image

1172
00:45:25,040 --> 00:45:29,359
quality by reducing these artifacts that

1173
00:45:27,119 --> 00:45:32,359
cause distortions in conventional CT

1174
00:45:29,359 --> 00:45:32,359
images.

1175
00:45:33,839 --> 00:45:38,079
Now let's discuss volume averaging and

1176
00:45:35,920 --> 00:45:39,599
various related artifacts. These

1177
00:45:38,079 --> 00:45:41,920
artifacts are not difficult to

1178
00:45:39,599 --> 00:45:44,880
understand conceptually, but effectively

1179
00:45:41,920 --> 00:45:47,280
removing them can be challenging.

1180
00:45:44,880 --> 00:45:49,280
In CT imaging, volume averaging occurs

1181
00:45:47,280 --> 00:45:51,200
because of finite detector element size

1182
00:45:49,280 --> 00:45:53,920
and limitations in the reconstruction

1183
00:45:51,200 --> 00:45:55,839
matrix's resolution.

1184
00:45:53,920 --> 00:45:57,760
These hardware elements are not small

1185
00:45:55,839 --> 00:46:00,000
enough to capture features smaller than

1186
00:45:57,760 --> 00:46:03,520
the detector size or the X-ray focal

1187
00:46:00,000 --> 00:46:05,920
spot. Imagine you have a fine detail, a

1188
00:46:03,520 --> 00:46:07,920
small yellow cross in an image. But your

1189
00:46:05,920 --> 00:46:09,920
image pixels are arranged in a grid and

1190
00:46:07,920 --> 00:46:11,839
this cross might fall entirely within a

1191
00:46:09,920 --> 00:46:14,160
single pixel.

1192
00:46:11,839 --> 00:46:16,319
That one pixel will then represent an

1193
00:46:14,160 --> 00:46:18,800
average of all the structures within it.

1194
00:46:16,319 --> 00:46:22,480
So the small cross may simply blend into

1195
00:46:18,800 --> 00:46:24,160
a larger, less distinct area.

1196
00:46:22,480 --> 00:46:26,160
For example, if the cross is in one

1197
00:46:24,160 --> 00:46:28,079
pixel and that pixel appears as a light

1198
00:46:26,160 --> 00:46:30,400
yellow block, you cannot resolve the

1199
00:46:28,079 --> 00:46:32,079
cross's true shape.

1200
00:46:30,400 --> 00:46:35,440
This phenomenon is called volume

1201
00:46:32,079 --> 00:46:37,359
averaging. In CT images, various factors

1202
00:46:35,440 --> 00:46:40,319
cause blurring and averaging effects

1203
00:46:37,359 --> 00:46:42,400
causing images to look less sharp.

1204
00:46:40,319 --> 00:46:44,880
Detector pixels have a limited size and

1205
00:46:42,400 --> 00:46:46,720
the X-ray sourc's focal spot is not a

1206
00:46:44,880 --> 00:46:50,079
perfect point but has an elliptical

1207
00:46:46,720 --> 00:46:52,000
shape which contributes to image blur.

1208
00:46:50,079 --> 00:46:53,920
To partially counteract this, image

1209
00:46:52,000 --> 00:46:56,160
processing techniques like debluring and

1210
00:46:53,920 --> 00:46:58,800
deconvolution are applied recovering

1211
00:46:56,160 --> 00:47:00,960
some lost resolution. This relates to

1212
00:46:58,800 --> 00:47:03,520
our earlier study of linear systems and

1213
00:47:00,960 --> 00:47:05,359
convolution in imaging.

1214
00:47:03,520 --> 00:47:07,680
So volume averaging is a fundamental

1215
00:47:05,359 --> 00:47:11,800
problem intrinsic to CT imaging limiting

1216
00:47:07,680 --> 00:47:11,800
the spatial resolution achievable.

1217
00:47:13,599 --> 00:47:18,160
Next we have what are called metal

1218
00:47:15,280 --> 00:47:20,319
artifacts. Inside the human body you may

1219
00:47:18,160 --> 00:47:22,880
have metal implants like pacemakers or

1220
00:47:20,319 --> 00:47:24,800
dental fillings. Metal is much denser

1221
00:47:22,880 --> 00:47:26,720
than bone so it can block X-rays

1222
00:47:24,800 --> 00:47:28,960
effectively.

1223
00:47:26,720 --> 00:47:31,520
Because of this, X-rays cannot penetrate

1224
00:47:28,960 --> 00:47:33,280
metal implants well. The attenuated

1225
00:47:31,520 --> 00:47:35,599
signal behind the metal may be very

1226
00:47:33,280 --> 00:47:37,280
noisy or even just noise without a clear

1227
00:47:35,599 --> 00:47:38,880
signal.

1228
00:47:37,280 --> 00:47:40,880
When you perform filtered back

1229
00:47:38,880 --> 00:47:42,560
projection reconstruction in such cases,

1230
00:47:40,880 --> 00:47:45,280
the results are often poor, showing

1231
00:47:42,560 --> 00:47:47,359
streaking artifacts.

1232
00:47:45,280 --> 00:47:49,599
This happens because the line integral

1233
00:47:47,359 --> 00:47:52,319
passing through the metal is very long

1234
00:47:49,599 --> 00:47:54,319
with many photons blocked or scattered.

1235
00:47:52,319 --> 00:47:56,480
Beam hardening and other effects further

1236
00:47:54,319 --> 00:47:58,400
degrade the signal.

1237
00:47:56,480 --> 00:48:00,400
As a result, the detected signals are

1238
00:47:58,400 --> 00:48:03,040
compromised with a low signal to noise

1239
00:48:00,400 --> 00:48:06,560
ratio. When estimating line integrals,

1240
00:48:03,040 --> 00:48:08,400
the data is inconsistent and inaccurate.

1241
00:48:06,560 --> 00:48:10,640
Filtered back projection on such

1242
00:48:08,400 --> 00:48:12,640
corrupted data generates stripe like

1243
00:48:10,640 --> 00:48:14,880
artifacts in the images, which is a

1244
00:48:12,640 --> 00:48:16,960
typical manifestation of metal artifacts

1245
00:48:14,880 --> 00:48:18,960
in CT.

1246
00:48:16,960 --> 00:48:21,119
Reducing metal artifacts is an important

1247
00:48:18,960 --> 00:48:22,880
research topic and multiple algorithms

1248
00:48:21,119 --> 00:48:25,880
have been developed though it remains

1249
00:48:22,880 --> 00:48:25,880
challenging.

1250
00:48:27,440 --> 00:48:31,520
The last topic I'm going to explain is

1251
00:48:29,200 --> 00:48:33,599
motion artifacts. Imagine you have a

1252
00:48:31,520 --> 00:48:36,079
phantom object like this one viewed at

1253
00:48:33,599 --> 00:48:38,720
angle zero. The phantom has a taiigi

1254
00:48:36,079 --> 00:48:40,559
pattern, a yin-yang shape.

1255
00:48:38,720 --> 00:48:42,559
As you slowly increase the projection

1256
00:48:40,559 --> 00:48:44,559
angle, the x-ray source rotates around

1257
00:48:42,559 --> 00:48:47,040
the phantom.

1258
00:48:44,559 --> 00:48:49,599
Over time, the data you collect changes.

1259
00:48:47,040 --> 00:48:51,680
When the angle reaches 90°, the phantom

1260
00:48:49,599 --> 00:48:53,839
appears compressed, shrunk to a minimum

1261
00:48:51,680 --> 00:48:56,720
in the projection.

1262
00:48:53,839 --> 00:48:58,960
As the source continues rotating at 180

1263
00:48:56,720 --> 00:49:00,640
degrees, the phantom appears expanded

1264
00:48:58,960 --> 00:49:02,400
again.

1265
00:49:00,640 --> 00:49:05,839
This back and forth pattern creates a

1266
00:49:02,400 --> 00:49:07,599
sinosoidal oscillation in the data.

1267
00:49:05,839 --> 00:49:09,520
This example illustrates what happens

1268
00:49:07,599 --> 00:49:11,839
when the object isn't stationary but

1269
00:49:09,520 --> 00:49:14,000
moves periodically. You gather

1270
00:49:11,839 --> 00:49:17,119
projection data while the object moves

1271
00:49:14,000 --> 00:49:18,880
according to this periodic motion.

1272
00:49:17,119 --> 00:49:20,960
If you then apply filtered back

1273
00:49:18,880 --> 00:49:23,200
projection to reconstruct images from

1274
00:49:20,960 --> 00:49:25,280
this data, you get motion artifacts,

1275
00:49:23,200 --> 00:49:28,480
blurring, or distortion caused by the

1276
00:49:25,280 --> 00:49:30,160
object's movement during acquisition.

1277
00:49:28,480 --> 00:49:31,920
However, if you know the object's motion

1278
00:49:30,160 --> 00:49:33,359
trajectory, you can incorporate that

1279
00:49:31,920 --> 00:49:35,920
information into iterative

1280
00:49:33,359 --> 00:49:37,760
reconstruction algorithms. With this

1281
00:49:35,920 --> 00:49:39,839
additional information, it is possible

1282
00:49:37,760 --> 00:49:41,599
to recover accurate images despite

1283
00:49:39,839 --> 00:49:43,599
motion.

1284
00:49:41,599 --> 00:49:45,599
Tommographic reconstruction remains

1285
00:49:43,599 --> 00:49:47,920
feasible provided you have either

1286
00:49:45,599 --> 00:49:51,839
explicit knowledge of motion or use

1287
00:49:47,920 --> 00:49:53,760
algorithms that infer motion implicitly.

1288
00:49:51,839 --> 00:49:55,680
Due to time constraints in this lecture,

1289
00:49:53,760 --> 00:49:58,800
we won't delve deeply into motion

1290
00:49:55,680 --> 00:50:01,280
artifact correction algorithms

1291
00:49:58,800 --> 00:50:03,440
for cardiac imaging. One way to reduce

1292
00:50:01,280 --> 00:50:06,000
motion artifacts is through hardware

1293
00:50:03,440 --> 00:50:07,920
performing ultraast scanning so the

1294
00:50:06,000 --> 00:50:10,160
heart doesn't have significant motion

1295
00:50:07,920 --> 00:50:12,160
during image acquisition. This

1296
00:50:10,160 --> 00:50:14,079
instantaneous imaging freezes the

1297
00:50:12,160 --> 00:50:16,800
cardiac structure, minimizing motion

1298
00:50:14,079 --> 00:50:18,319
blur.

1299
00:50:16,800 --> 00:50:20,640
Another way is through smart

1300
00:50:18,319 --> 00:50:22,480
computational algorithms that predict

1301
00:50:20,640 --> 00:50:25,440
motion and correct for it during

1302
00:50:22,480 --> 00:50:27,760
reconstruction. Often cardiac CT

1303
00:50:25,440 --> 00:50:30,079
patients are given beta blockers to slow

1304
00:50:27,760 --> 00:50:31,839
the heart rate, reducing motion during

1305
00:50:30,079 --> 00:50:33,920
scanning.

1306
00:50:31,839 --> 00:50:35,920
Additionally, kinetic acquisition can be

1307
00:50:33,920 --> 00:50:37,839
synchronized with the ECG signal,

1308
00:50:35,920 --> 00:50:40,880
coupling image data with specific

1309
00:50:37,839 --> 00:50:43,119
cardiac phases. The ECG indicates when

1310
00:50:40,880 --> 00:50:44,800
the heart is largest or smallest. You

1311
00:50:43,119 --> 00:50:46,480
can select data segments corresponding

1312
00:50:44,800 --> 00:50:49,760
to the heart's maximum volume and

1313
00:50:46,480 --> 00:50:52,000
reconstruct images at that phase.

1314
00:50:49,760 --> 00:50:53,839
Similarly, you regroup data segments for

1315
00:50:52,000 --> 00:50:56,319
the minimum volume phase and reconstruct

1316
00:50:53,839 --> 00:50:58,079
images accordingly. This approach allows

1317
00:50:56,319 --> 00:51:00,720
reconstruction of a beating heart in

1318
00:50:58,079 --> 00:51:02,640
different cardiac phases.

1319
00:51:00,720 --> 00:51:04,559
This gives a rough idea of how motion

1320
00:51:02,640 --> 00:51:07,559
artifacts are managed in dynamic organ

1321
00:51:04,559 --> 00:51:07,559
imaging.

1322
00:51:09,040 --> 00:51:13,599
Let's finish with some important points

1323
00:51:10,720 --> 00:51:16,000
about X-ray radiation. X-rays are much

1324
00:51:13,599 --> 00:51:17,920
more energetic than visible light. We

1325
00:51:16,000 --> 00:51:20,079
experience visible light all the time,

1326
00:51:17,920 --> 00:51:22,240
sunshine for example, which is generally

1327
00:51:20,079 --> 00:51:23,760
considered healthy.

1328
00:51:22,240 --> 00:51:26,480
But X-rays have much shorter

1329
00:51:23,760 --> 00:51:29,839
wavelengths, roughly from 0.1 nanome to

1330
00:51:26,480 --> 00:51:31,680
10 nanome and much higher energy.

1331
00:51:29,839 --> 00:51:33,680
Because X-ray photons carry so much

1332
00:51:31,680 --> 00:51:36,400
energy, they can break molecular bonds

1333
00:51:33,680 --> 00:51:38,160
in biological tissues. This damage can

1334
00:51:36,400 --> 00:51:40,720
potentially cause genetic mutations

1335
00:51:38,160 --> 00:51:42,880
which are often harmful.

1336
00:51:40,720 --> 00:51:44,559
In the long term, such mutations may

1337
00:51:42,880 --> 00:51:47,280
increase the risk of cancer or other

1338
00:51:44,559 --> 00:51:49,680
genetic diseases. There is some debate,

1339
00:51:47,280 --> 00:51:51,680
though. Some argue that small doses of

1340
00:51:49,680 --> 00:51:54,000
radiation might even be beneficial,

1341
00:51:51,680 --> 00:51:57,040
similar to exercise, causing small

1342
00:51:54,000 --> 00:51:58,880
stress that strengthens the body.

1343
00:51:57,040 --> 00:52:01,359
In this view, controlled moderate

1344
00:51:58,880 --> 00:52:03,359
radiation exposure acts like a vitamin,

1345
00:52:01,359 --> 00:52:05,440
gently challenging the body to repair

1346
00:52:03,359 --> 00:52:07,599
and strengthen itself. While that

1347
00:52:05,440 --> 00:52:10,480
perspective exists in some articles, the

1348
00:52:07,599 --> 00:52:12,640
general consensus is caution.

1349
00:52:10,480 --> 00:52:15,119
As CT imaging, especially advanced

1350
00:52:12,640 --> 00:52:17,280
methods like helical CT for lung cancer

1351
00:52:15,119 --> 00:52:20,880
screening becomes more widespread,

1352
00:52:17,280 --> 00:52:23,200
concerns about radiation dose increase.

1353
00:52:20,880 --> 00:52:25,760
Patients receive higher cumulative doses

1354
00:52:23,200 --> 00:52:27,839
and this raises public health questions.

1355
00:52:25,760 --> 00:52:29,839
So it is crucial to focus on how we can

1356
00:52:27,839 --> 00:52:33,559
reduce radiation dose and how we measure

1357
00:52:29,839 --> 00:52:33,559
it in the first place.

1358
00:52:34,960 --> 00:52:38,480
Let me explain the basic concepts from

1359
00:52:36,800 --> 00:52:40,160
your green book regarding radiation

1360
00:52:38,480 --> 00:52:42,079
dose.

1361
00:52:40,160 --> 00:52:44,160
X-ray radiation is a form of

1362
00:52:42,079 --> 00:52:45,839
electromagnetic radiation and when it

1363
00:52:44,160 --> 00:52:48,400
passes through your body energy is

1364
00:52:45,839 --> 00:52:51,680
deposited into your tissues. This energy

1365
00:52:48,400 --> 00:52:53,440
deposited is called the absorbed dose.

1366
00:52:51,680 --> 00:52:55,440
The absorbed dose is a physical

1367
00:52:53,440 --> 00:52:57,920
measurement of energy per unit mass of

1368
00:52:55,440 --> 00:53:00,720
tissue expressed in grays. One gray

1369
00:52:57,920 --> 00:53:02,400
equals 1 jewel per kilogram.

1370
00:53:00,720 --> 00:53:04,240
This is the fundamental concept of

1371
00:53:02,400 --> 00:53:07,839
radiation dose, the energy your body

1372
00:53:04,240 --> 00:53:09,760
absorbs from X-rays.

1373
00:53:07,839 --> 00:53:11,520
However, from a health perspective, the

1374
00:53:09,760 --> 00:53:13,280
effective dose equivalent is more

1375
00:53:11,520 --> 00:53:15,359
relevant.

1376
00:53:13,280 --> 00:53:17,680
To calculate this, the human body is

1377
00:53:15,359 --> 00:53:19,520
divided into multiple organs or regions

1378
00:53:17,680 --> 00:53:22,720
because different organs have different

1379
00:53:19,520 --> 00:53:24,640
sensitivities to radiation.

1380
00:53:22,720 --> 00:53:26,960
Some tissues like those involved in

1381
00:53:24,640 --> 00:53:29,359
reproduction or rapidly regenerating

1382
00:53:26,960 --> 00:53:31,599
cells are more sensitive to radiation

1383
00:53:29,359 --> 00:53:33,359
damage. Therefore, younger women,

1384
00:53:31,599 --> 00:53:35,200
children, and reproductive organs

1385
00:53:33,359 --> 00:53:37,760
require special caution to avoid

1386
00:53:35,200 --> 00:53:39,680
excessive exposure.

1387
00:53:37,760 --> 00:53:42,800
This sensitivity is accounted for by

1388
00:53:39,680 --> 00:53:45,040
waiting factors denoted by W. The dose

1389
00:53:42,800 --> 00:53:46,880
equivalent represented by H combines the

1390
00:53:45,040 --> 00:53:49,839
absorbed dose with factors related to

1391
00:53:46,880 --> 00:53:51,839
the type and energy of radiation.

1392
00:53:49,839 --> 00:53:53,839
Higher energy photons or particles

1393
00:53:51,839 --> 00:53:56,960
generally cause more biological damage

1394
00:53:53,839 --> 00:53:59,280
per unit of absorbed energy.

1395
00:53:56,960 --> 00:54:01,359
For example, X-rays, gamma rays, and

1396
00:53:59,280 --> 00:54:04,000
alpha particles differ in their relative

1397
00:54:01,359 --> 00:54:06,480
biological effectiveness. To account for

1398
00:54:04,000 --> 00:54:08,400
this, a quality factor QF is applied to

1399
00:54:06,480 --> 00:54:11,040
quantify the biological impact of

1400
00:54:08,400 --> 00:54:13,359
different radiation types.

1401
00:54:11,040 --> 00:54:15,200
For X-ray CT, the QF is normalized to

1402
00:54:13,359 --> 00:54:17,359
one, but other radiation types have

1403
00:54:15,200 --> 00:54:19,280
different values.

1404
00:54:17,359 --> 00:54:21,599
